arXiv:2410.07756v2 [math.CO] 26 Oct 2024</div>
</div><div class="ltx_page_content">
<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">Graphs with nonnegative resistance curvature</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Karel Devriendt
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span><span id="id1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">University of Oxford, UK</span>
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">This article introduces and studies a new class of graphs motivated by discrete curvature. We call a graph <em id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">resistance nonnegative</em> if there exists a distribution on its spanning trees such that every vertex has expected degree at most two in a random spanning tree; these are precisely the graphs that admit a metric with nonnegative resistance curvature, a discrete curvature introduced by Devriendt and Lambiotte. We show that this class of graphs lies between Hamiltonian and <math id="abstract1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>-tough graphs and, surprisingly, that a graph is resistance nonnegative if and only if its twice-dilated matching polytope intersects the interior of its spanning tree polytope. We study further characterizations and basic properties of resistance nonnegative graphs and pose several questions for future research.</p>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<section id="S1.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.1 </span>Motivation</h3>

<div id="S1.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>, the author and Lambiotte introduced a new notion of discrete scalar curvature on the vertices of a graph, based on the geometry of effective resistances. For a simple graph <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G=(V,E)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G=(V,E)</annotation></semantics></math> with edge weights <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, the <em id="S1.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">effective resistance</em> <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> between two adjacent vertices is proportional to the <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math>-weighted fraction of all spanning trees that contain the edge between them:</p>
<table id="S1.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{uv}=c_{uv}^{-1}\frac{\sum_{T\ni uv}\prod_{t\in T}c_{t}}{\sum_{T}\prod_{t\in T}c_{t}}\quad\quad\text{~for $uv\in E$,}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>∋</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0.167em">∏</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>T</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0.167em">∏</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mrow><mtext> for </mtext><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><mtext>,</mtext></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{uv}=c_{uv}^{-1}\frac{\sum_{T\ni uv}\prod_{t\in T}c_{t}}{\sum_{T}\prod_{t\in T}c_{t}}\quad\quad\text{~for $uv\in E$,}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> runs over all spanning trees in <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>. The effective resistance originates from electrical circuit analysis and potential theory and it has a rich combinatorial, algebraic and geometric theory; see for instance <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>. The <em id="S1.SS1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">resistance curvature</em> <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> is a discrete scalar curvature on the vertices of a graph, defined in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite> as:</p>
<table id="S1.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{v}(c)=1-\frac{1}{2}\sum_{uv\in E}\omega_{uv}c_{uv}\quad\quad\text{~for $v\in V$}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mrow><mtext> for </mtext><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{v}(c)=1-\frac{1}{2}\sum_{uv\in E}\omega_{uv}c_{uv}\quad\quad\text{~for $v\in V$}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">As in differential geometry, graphs with nonnegative resistance curvature have a lot of nice properties <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>, <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite> and we note in particular a strong structural theorem for positively curved graphs; a graph <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is called <em id="S1.SS1.p1.3.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic"><math id="S1.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>-tough</em> if for every set of vertices <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\subset V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\subset V</annotation></semantics></math> whose removal disconnects the graph, the graph <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G-U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G-U</annotation></semantics></math> has at most <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|U|/t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|U|/t</annotation></semantics></math> components. The following is implied by a result of Fiedler in simplex geometry <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>, Thm. 3.4.18]</cite> and another proof is given in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, Thm. 6.31]</cite>:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1.1</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>)<span id="S1.Thmtheorem1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Connected weighted graphs with positive resistance curvature are <math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>-tough.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In this result, we point out that positive resistance curvature depends on a choice of edge weights <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math>, while <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>-tough is a purely combinatorial property that only depends on the graph data <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G=(V,E)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G=(V,E)</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, if one can find some edge weights for which <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, then Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1.1 ([]). ‣ 1.1 Motivation ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Graphs with nonnegative resistance curvature" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a> applies and the graph is 1-tough. This observation naturally motives the following question:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_question">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Question 1.2</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For which graphs <math id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> does there exist a choice of edge weights <math id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively <math id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>? We call these graphs <em id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">resistance nonnegative</em> (RN) and <em id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">resistance positive</em> (RP). A graph which is RN but not RP is called <em id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">strictly resistance nonnegative</em> (SRN).</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Geometrically, by thinking of edge weights as inverse edge lengths, this question can be reformulated as “which graphs admit a metric with nonnegative/positive scalar curvature”. Replacing “graph” by “manifold”, this parallels a classical question in differential geometry <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite>. With the definitions in Question <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem2" title="Question 1.2. ‣ 1.1 Motivation ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Graphs with nonnegative resistance curvature" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.2</span></a>, we can turn Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1.1 ([]). ‣ 1.1 Motivation ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Graphs with nonnegative resistance curvature" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a> into a purely structural result.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1.3</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Connected resistance positive graphs are 1-tough.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In this article, we will answer Question <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem2" title="Question 1.2. ‣ 1.1 Motivation ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Graphs with nonnegative resistance curvature" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.2</span></a> and study characterizations and properties of RN, RP and SRN graphs. We formulate several questions for future research.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.2 </span>Main results</h3>

<div id="S1.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As a first main result, we show that RN, RP and SRN graphs can be characterized in terms of the existence of certain distributions on their spanning trees; a distribution is <em id="S1.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">positive</em> if every spanning tree is assigned a non-zero probability.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thminnercustomthm1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_innercustomthm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thminnercustomthm1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem </span><a href="#S3.Thmtheorem8" title="Theorem 3.8. ‣ 3 Resistance nonnegative graphs ‣ Graphs with nonnegative resistance curvature" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_bold"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.8</span></a></span><span id="Thminnercustomthm1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thminnercustomthm1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thminnercustomthm1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thminnercustomthm1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">A graph <math id="Thminnercustomthm1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is RN (resp. RP) if and only if there exists a positive distribution on the set of spanning trees of <math id="Thminnercustomthm1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, such that the expected degree of every vertex in a random spanning tree under this distribution is at most <math id="Thminnercustomthm1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> (resp. strictly smaller than <math id="Thminnercustomthm1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>).</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The actual statement of Theorem <a href="#Thminnercustomthm1" title="Theorem . ‣ 1.2 Main results ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Graphs with nonnegative resistance curvature" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.2</span></a> in the main text is stronger and allows for other types of distributions. Our proof makes use of the fact that <math id="S1.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(c_{uv}\omega_{uv})_{uv\in E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(c_{uv}\omega_{uv})_{uv\in E}</annotation></semantics></math> are marginal probabilities of a so-called log-linear distribution on spanning trees induced by <math id="S1.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math>. By classical results in statistics on log-linear distributions, as one ranges over all possible weights <math id="S1.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;c&lt;\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;c&lt;\infty</annotation></semantics></math> these marginal probabilities trace out the (interior of the) set of marginals of all possible distributions on spanning trees. This set is a polytope <math id="S1.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(G)\subset\mathbb{R}^{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>E</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(G)\subset\mathbb{R}^{E}</annotation></semantics></math> and is called the <em id="S1.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">spanning tree polytope</em> of <math id="S1.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>. Since the expected degree constraints are affine, a practical consequence of Theorem <a href="#Thminnercustomthm1" title="Theorem . ‣ 1.2 Main results ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Graphs with nonnegative resistance curvature" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.2</span></a> is that computing the RN property of a graph is a linear program on <math id="S1.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(G)</annotation></semantics></math>.

<br class="ltx_break"> 
<br class="ltx_break">As a second main result, we give a geometric characterization of RN graphs via the intersection of two polytopes associated to a graph; recall that a matching is a subset of edges <math id="S1.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M\subseteq E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M\subseteq E</annotation></semantics></math> which covers every vertex at most once. The spanning tree polytope and <em id="S1.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">matching polytope</em> of <math id="S1.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> are</p>
<table id="S1.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(G):=\operatorname{conv}(e_{T}\mid T\text{~is a spanning tree})\subset\mathbb{R}^{E},\quad M(G):=\operatorname{conv}(e_{M}\mid M\text{~is a matching})\subset\mathbb{R}^{E}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>conv</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> is a spanning tree</mtext></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>E</mi></msup></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>conv</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> is a matching</mtext></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>E</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(G):=\operatorname{conv}(e_{T}\mid T\text{~is a spanning tree})\subset\mathbb{R}^{E},\quad M(G):=\operatorname{conv}(e_{M}\mid M\text{~is a matching})\subset\mathbb{R}^{E},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S1.SS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{T}=\sum_{i\in T}e_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{T}=\sum_{i\in T}e_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S1.SS2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> basis vectors of <math id="S1.SS2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>E</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{E}</annotation></semantics></math>. We use the notation <math id="S1.SS2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2M(G)=\{2x\mid x\in M(G)\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2M(G)=\{2x\mid x\in M(G)\}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thminnercustomthm2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_innercustomthm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thminnercustomthm2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem </span><a href="#S4.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 4.1. ‣ 4 Trees &amp; double matchings ‣ Graphs with nonnegative resistance curvature" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_bold"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a></span><span id="Thminnercustomthm2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thminnercustomthm2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thminnercustomthm2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thminnercustomthm2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">A graph <math id="Thminnercustomthm2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is RN if and only if <math id="Thminnercustomthm2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(G)^{\circ}\cap 2M(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(G)^{\circ}\cap 2M(G)</annotation></semantics></math> is non-empty.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Following this theorem, we initiate a study of the polytope <math id="S1.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta(G)=P(G)\cap 2M(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta(G)=P(G)\cap 2M(G)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thminnercustomprop1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_innercustomprop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thminnercustomprop1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition </span><a href="#S4.Thmtheorem6" title="Proposition 4.6. ‣ 4 Trees &amp; double matchings ‣ Graphs with nonnegative resistance curvature" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_bold"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.6</span></a></span><span id="Thminnercustomprop1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thminnercustomprop1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thminnercustomprop1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thminnercustomprop1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The integer points of <math id="Thminnercustomprop1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta(G)</annotation></semantics></math> are indicator vectors of Hamiltonian paths in <math id="Thminnercustomprop1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">As a direction for future research, we propose to study Ehrhart-type questions for this polytope: what can be said about the integer points of <math id="S1.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\Theta(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\Theta(G)</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S1.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\in\mathbb{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\in\mathbb{N}</annotation></semantics></math>? Combinatorially, this requires counting arrangements of double matchings that give arrangements of spanning trees.

<br class="ltx_break"> 
<br class="ltx_break">As a third line of results, we study some operations on graphs and their effect on resistance nonnegativity. We show that adding edges preserves RP, that ‘Kron reduction’ (Definition <a href="#S6.Thmtheorem7" title="Definition 6.7 (Kron reduction). ‣ 6.2 Kron reduction ‣ 6 Graph operations ‣ Graphs with nonnegative resistance curvature" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.7</span></a>) preserves both RP and RN but not SRN and that ‘circle inversion’ (Definition <a href="#S6.Thmtheorem12" title="Definition 6.12 (circle inversion, []). ‣ 6.3 Circle inversion ‣ 6 Graph operations ‣ Graphs with nonnegative resistance curvature" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.12</span></a>) preserves RN. One application of these closure results is the following theorem:</p>
</div>
<div id="Thminnercustomthm3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_innercustomthm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thminnercustomthm3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem </span><a href="#S6.Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 6.2. ‣ 6.1 Subgraphs ‣ 6 Graph operations ‣ Graphs with nonnegative resistance curvature" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_bold"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.2</span></a></span><span id="Thminnercustomthm3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thminnercustomthm3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thminnercustomthm3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thminnercustomthm3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Hamiltonian graphs are resistance positive, but the converse is not always true.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Graph toughness was introduced by Chvátal to study Hamiltonian graphs and <math id="S1.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>-tough does not imply Hamiltonicity; see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>. We ask whether the class of RP graphs lies strictly between Hamiltonian and <math id="S1.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>-tough graphs and whether every non-Hamiltonian <math id="S1.SS2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>-tough graph is RP.

<br class="ltx_break"> 
<br class="ltx_break">Finally, we discuss two further characterizations of RN and RP graphs. Similar to encoding the RN conditions for a graph <math id="S1.SS2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> by affine inequalities on the space <math id="S1.SS2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(G)\subset\mathbb{R}^{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>E</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(G)\subset\mathbb{R}^{E}</annotation></semantics></math> that parametrizes all effective resistances in a graph, we introduce two other parametrization spaces <math id="S1.SS2.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{K}(G)\subset\mathbb{R}^{V\times V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{K}(G)\subset\mathbb{R}^{V\times V}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SS2.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}(G)\subset[0,1]^{2^{V}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>V</mi></msup></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}(G)\subset[0,1]^{2^{V}}</annotation></semantics></math> on which the RN conditions are affine inequalities. Here, <math id="S1.SS2.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{K}(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{K}(G)</annotation></semantics></math> is the set of all inverse resistance matrices of a graph and <math id="S1.SS2.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}(G)</annotation></semantics></math> the space of all resistance capacities, these are certain set function on <math id="S1.SS2.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2^{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>V</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2^{V}</annotation></semantics></math> induced by effective resistances. The conjectured inequalities on <math id="S1.SS2.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}(G)</annotation></semantics></math> are the submodularity inequalities, which are known to be necessary for RN.

<br class="ltx_break"> 
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="S1.SS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Organization:</span> In Section <a href="#S2" title="2 Relative resistance &amp; the spanning tree polytope ‣ Graphs with nonnegative resistance curvature" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> we discuss effective resistances, their relation to distributions on spanning trees, and the spanning tree polytope <math id="S1.SS2.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(G)</annotation></semantics></math>. In Section <a href="#S3" title="3 Resistance nonnegative graphs ‣ Graphs with nonnegative resistance curvature" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, we define RN, RP and SRN graphs, give some examples and prove a first characterization result, Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem8" title="Theorem 3.8. ‣ 3 Resistance nonnegative graphs ‣ Graphs with nonnegative resistance curvature" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.8</span></a>. Section <a href="#S4" title="4 Trees &amp; double matchings ‣ Graphs with nonnegative resistance curvature" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> deals with the second characterization result, Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 4.1. ‣ 4 Trees &amp; double matchings ‣ Graphs with nonnegative resistance curvature" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>, which relates resistance nonnegativity and matchings. We initiate the study of the polytope <math id="S1.SS2.p5.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta(G)</annotation></semantics></math>. In Section <a href="#S5" title="5 Algebraic characterization ‣ Graphs with nonnegative resistance curvature" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> we prove a third characterization result, Corollary <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem4" title="Corollary 5.4. ‣ 5 Algebraic characterization ‣ Graphs with nonnegative resistance curvature" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.4</span></a>, in terms of the space of inverse resistance matrices <math id="S1.SS2.p5.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{K}(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{K}(G)</annotation></semantics></math>. Section <a href="#S6" title="6 Graph operations ‣ Graphs with nonnegative resistance curvature" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> deals with three graph operations and their influence on resistance nonnegativity. Lastly, Section <a href="#S7" title="7 Nonnegativity from submodularity ‣ Graphs with nonnegative resistance curvature" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> conjectures a fourth characterization, Conjecture <a href="#S7.Thmtheorem7" title="Conjecture 7.7. ‣ 7 Nonnegativity from submodularity ‣ Graphs with nonnegative resistance curvature" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.7</span></a>, which relates resistance nonnegativity to submodularity of the resistance capacity.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Relative resistance &amp; the spanning tree polytope</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Throughout the article, we will consider finite graphs <math id="S2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G=(V,E)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G=(V,E)</annotation></semantics></math> which are simple (no multi-edges), loopless (no <math id="S2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="vv" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">vv</annotation></semantics></math> edges) and connected, unless stated otherwise. A graph is <em id="S2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">biconnected</em> if <math id="S2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G-v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G-v</annotation></semantics></math> is connected for every <math id="S2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in V</annotation></semantics></math>. Many of the presented results will hold in a more general setting, but this is left for later development. The following definition is central in this article:</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2.1</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(spanning tree)<span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">A <em id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">spanning tree</em> of <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is a maximal acyclic subset of edges.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In other words, a spanning tree is a set of edges <math id="S2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\subseteq E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\subseteq E</annotation></semantics></math>, such that <math id="S2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> contains no cycles in <math id="S2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> but adding any further edge from <math id="S2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E\setminus T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>∖</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E\setminus T</annotation></semantics></math> creates a cycle. Every spanning tree in a connected graph has <math id="S2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|V|-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|V|-1</annotation></semantics></math> edges. We will use the fact that spanning trees form a matroid <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>, Ch. 13]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2.2</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(spanning tree polytope)<span id="S2.Thmtheorem2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The <em id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">spanning tree polytope</em> of a graph <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(G)=\operatorname{conv}(e_{T}\mid T\text{~is a spanning tree})\subset\mathbb{R}^{E}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>conv</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"> is a spanning tree</mtext></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>E</mi></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(G)=\operatorname{conv}(e_{T}\mid T\text{~is a spanning tree})\subset\mathbb{R}^{E}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_example">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Example 2.3</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>-cycle has vertices <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u,v,w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u,v,w</annotation></semantics></math> and edges <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="uv,vw,uw" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">uv,vw,uw</annotation></semantics></math>. It has <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math> spanning trees <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{uv,vw\},\{vw,uw\},\{uv,uw\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{uv,vw\},\{vw,uw\},\{uv,uw\}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(G)</annotation></semantics></math> is a triangle in <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{3}</annotation></semantics></math> with vertices <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1,1,0),(0,1,1),(1,0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1,1,0),(0,1,1),(1,0,1)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 2.4</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(open spanning tree polytope)<span id="S2.Thmtheorem4.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The spanning tree polytope is not full-dimensional in <math id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>E</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{E}</annotation></semantics></math> by Foster’s theorems (see below). For this reason, when referring to the <em id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">open spanning tree polytope</em> <math id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(G)^{\circ}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(G)^{\circ}</annotation></semantics></math>, we mean its relative interior in the affine subspace in which it lives. In particular, the spanning tree polytope of a tree <math id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is a point <math id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{T}</annotation></semantics></math>, and so is its relative interior. This is important when <math id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is a path, for instance in Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 4.1. ‣ 4 Trees &amp; double matchings ‣ Graphs with nonnegative resistance curvature" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In a weighted graph, one associates finite positive weights <math id="S2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(c_{e})_{e\in E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(c_{e})_{e\in E}</annotation></semantics></math> to the edges of <math id="S2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>. Rather than working with the effective resistance <math id="S2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{uv}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{uv}</annotation></semantics></math> as introduced in (<a href="#S1.E1" title="In 1.1 Motivation ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Graphs with nonnegative resistance curvature" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>), we consider the <em id="S2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">relative resistance</em> <math id="S2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{e}=\omega_{e}c_{e}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{e}=\omega_{e}c_{e}</annotation></semantics></math> defined on the edges of a graph. We recall its definition.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2.5</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(relative resistance)<span id="S2.Thmtheorem5.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The <em id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">relative resistance</em> of an edge <math id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e\in E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e\in E</annotation></semantics></math> is</span></p>
<table id="S2.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{e}(c)=\frac{\sum_{T\ni e}\prod_{t\in T}c_{t}}{\sum_{T}\prod_{t\in T}c_{t}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>∋</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0.167em">∏</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>T</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0.167em">∏</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{e}(c)=\frac{\sum_{T\ni e}\prod_{t\in T}c_{t}}{\sum_{T}\prod_{t\in T}c_{t}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The homogeneous polynomial <math id="S2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{G}(c)=\sum_{T}\prod_{t\in T}c_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0em">∑</mo><mi>T</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mo>∏</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{G}(c)=\sum_{T}\prod_{t\in T}c_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> in the denominator of (<a href="#S2.E2" title="In Definition 2.5 (relative resistance). ‣ 2 Relative resistance &amp; the spanning tree polytope ‣ Graphs with nonnegative resistance curvature" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) is called the Kirchhoff polynomial of <math id="S2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, and we note that <math id="S2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{e}\cdot Z_{G}=c_{e}\cdot\partial Z_{G}/\partial c_{e}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>G</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.055em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>G</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">/</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{e}\cdot Z_{G}=c_{e}\cdot\partial Z_{G}/\partial c_{e}</annotation></semantics></math>. From Euler’s homogeneous function theorem, one recovers the following classical result in electrical circuit analysis:</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 2.6</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Foster’s theorem <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>)<span id="S2.Thmtheorem6.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.Thmtheorem6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{e\in E}r_{e}(c)=|V|-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{e\in E}r_{e}(c)=|V|-1</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.Thmtheorem6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> for any <math id="S2.Thmtheorem6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;c&lt;\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;c&lt;\infty</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">We strengthen Foster’s theorem to a localized version on the biconnected components of <math id="S2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 2.7</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(local Foster’s theorem)<span id="S2.Thmtheorem7.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.Thmtheorem7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{e\in E(U)}r_{e}(c)=|U|-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{e\in E(U)}r_{e}(c)=|U|-1</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.Thmtheorem7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> for any <math id="S2.Thmtheorem7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;c&lt;\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;c&lt;\infty</annotation></semantics></math>, whenever <math id="S2.Thmtheorem7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\subseteq V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\subseteq V</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.Thmtheorem7.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(U)\subseteq E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(U)\subseteq E</annotation></semantics></math> are the vertices and edges of a biconnected component of <math id="S2.Thmtheorem7.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof.</span>
This follows from (<a href="#S2.E2" title="In Definition 2.5 (relative resistance). ‣ 2 Relative resistance &amp; the spanning tree polytope ‣ Graphs with nonnegative resistance curvature" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) and the fact that every spanning tree has the same number of edges in every biconnected component, namely, the size of the component minus one. The biconnected components of a graph are the connected components of the corresponding cycle matroid.
∎</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Next, we consider the subset of <math id="S2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>E</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{E}</annotation></semantics></math> traced out by <math id="S2.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(r_{e}(c))_{e\in E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(r_{e}(c))_{e\in E}</annotation></semantics></math> as we range over all possible weights <math id="S2.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math>. This will turn out to be precisely the interior of the polytope <math id="S2.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(G)</annotation></semantics></math>. This generalizes Foster’s theorem, since it characterizes all possible tuples of relative resistances by a collection of affine equalities and inequalities in <math id="S2.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>. Our discussion will follow a probabilistic approach.

<br class="ltx_break"> 
<br class="ltx_break">Let <math id="S2.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}=\mathcal{T}(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}=\mathcal{T}(G)</annotation></semantics></math> be the collection of all spanning trees of <math id="S2.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S2.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{c}:\mathcal{T}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{c}:\mathcal{T}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> given by</p>
<table id="S2.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{c}(T)=\frac{\prod_{t\in T}c_{t}}{Z_{G}(c)}&gt;0\quad\quad\text{~for $T\in\mathcal{T}$,}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mo>∏</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mrow><mtext> for </mtext><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></mrow><mtext>,</mtext></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{c}(T)=\frac{\prod_{t\in T}c_{t}}{Z_{G}(c)}&gt;0\quad\quad\text{~for $T\in\mathcal{T}$,}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p7.2" class="ltx_p">is a distribution on spanning trees, and we call <math id="S2.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{c}(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{c}(T)</annotation></semantics></math> the probability of <math id="S2.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>. Since this distribution is of the form <math id="S2.p7.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{c}(T)\propto\operatorname{exp}(\sum_{t\in T}y_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∝</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{c}(T)\propto\operatorname{exp}(\sum_{t\in T}y_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> under the transformation <math id="S2.p7.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{t}=\log c_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{t}=\log c_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, this is also called a <em id="S2.p7.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">log-linear distribution</em> or Gibbs distribution. We consider two further types of distributions on <math id="S2.p7.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}</annotation></semantics></math>, with increasing generality. First, a <em id="S2.p7.2.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">positive distribution</em> assigns a positive probability to each spanning tree. For the second type, we need the following class of subsets of spanning trees:</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem8" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 2.8</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem8.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The following are equivalent for a subset of spanning trees <math id="S2.Thmtheorem8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}\subseteq\mathcal{T}(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}\subseteq\mathcal{T}(G)</annotation></semantics></math>:</span></p>
<ol id="S2.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S2.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{conv}(e_{T}\mid T\in\mathcal{F})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>conv</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{conv}(e_{T}\mid T\in\mathcal{F})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> intersects </span><math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(G)^{\circ}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(G)^{\circ}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">;</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{conv}(e_{T}\mid T\in\mathcal{F})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>conv</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{conv}(e_{T}\mid T\in\mathcal{F})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is not contained in a proper face of </span><math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(G)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">;</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\min(w(T)\mid T\in\mathcal{F})&lt;\max(w(T)\mid T\in\mathcal{T})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\min(w(T)\mid T\in\mathcal{F})&lt;\max(w(T)\mid T\in\mathcal{T})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> for all </span><math id="S2.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w\in\mathbb{R}^{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>E</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w\in\mathbb{R}^{E}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">,</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem8.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem8.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="S2.Thmtheorem8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(T)=\sum_{t\in T}w_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(T)=\sum_{t\in T}w_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.Thmtheorem8.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> is not piecewise constant on the biconnected components of <math id="S2.Thmtheorem8.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>. These sets <math id="S2.Thmtheorem8.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> are called <em id="S2.Thmtheorem8.p1.2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">non-separable</em>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof.</span>
Equivalence of the first two statements follows immediately from the fact that <math id="S2.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(e_{T})_{T\in\mathcal{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(e_{T})_{T\in\mathcal{F}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a subset of the vertices of <math id="S2.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(G)</annotation></semantics></math> and that proper faces of a polytope have positive codimension. The third statement says that there is no linear function <math id="S2.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{w}:x\mapsto\langle w,x\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{w}:x\mapsto\langle w,x\rangle</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S2.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(G)</annotation></semantics></math> which is maximized on the whole set <math id="S2.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(e_{T})_{T\in\mathcal{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(e_{T})_{T\in\mathcal{F}}</annotation></semantics></math>. This is equivalent to saying that these points are not contained in a face of <math id="S2.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(G)</annotation></semantics></math>, which is statement 2. By Foster’s local theorem, it follows that <math id="S2.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{w}</annotation></semantics></math> is constant on the vertices of <math id="S2.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(G)</annotation></semantics></math> when <math id="S2.p8.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> is piecewise constant on biconnected components.
∎</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p9.1" class="ltx_p">A distribution on spanning trees is called <em id="S2.p9.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">non-separable</em> if it is supported on a non-separable subset of spanning trees. We note that non-separable <math id="S2.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\supset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⊃</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\supset</annotation></semantics></math> positive <math id="S2.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\supset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⊃</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\supset</annotation></semantics></math> log-linear. Finally, the tuple <math id="S2.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\sum_{T\ni e}\mu(T))_{e\in E}\in\mathbb{R}^{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>∋</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>E</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\sum_{T\ni e}\mu(T))_{e\in E}\in\mathbb{R}^{E}</annotation></semantics></math> are called the <em id="S2.p9.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">edge-marginals</em> of the distribution <math id="S2.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math>. These are the probabilities that a random spanning tree following <math id="S2.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math> contains each of the respective edges.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem9" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem9.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 2.9</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem9.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem9.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem9.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S2.Thmtheorem9.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> be a graph, then the following subsets of <math id="S2.Thmtheorem9.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>E</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{E}</annotation></semantics></math> coincide:</span></p>
<ol id="S2.I2" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S2.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S2.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">the open spanning tree polytope </span><math id="S2.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(G)^{\circ}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(G)^{\circ}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I2.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">;</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S2.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">edge marginals of non-separable distributions on </span><math id="S2.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}(G)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I2.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">;</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S2.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I2.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">edge marginals of positive distributions on </span><math id="S2.I2.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}(G)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I2.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">;</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I2.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">4.</span> 
<div id="S2.I2.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I2.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I2.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">edge marginals of log-linear distributions on </span><math id="S2.I2.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}(G)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I2.i4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">;</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I2.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">5.</span> 
<div id="S2.I2.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I2.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I2.i5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">relative resistances of the edges in </span><math id="S2.I2.i5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I2.i5.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, over all possible edge weights.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem9.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem9.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Every point in this subset corresponds to a unique choice of edge weights, up to common rescaling in the biconnected components of <math id="S2.Thmtheorem9.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p10" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof.</span>
The translation between probability and geometry goes via interpreting the probabilities <math id="S2.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu(T)</annotation></semantics></math> as convex coefficients of the vertices <math id="S2.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{T}</annotation></semantics></math>. The edge marginals of a distribution are then precisely the coordinates of the point in <math id="S2.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(G)</annotation></semantics></math> determined by these convex coefficients:</p>
<table id="S2.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Big(\sum_{T}\mu(T)e_{T}\Big)_{i}\,=\,\sum_{T}\mu(T)(e_{T})_{i}\,=\,\sum_{T\ni i}\mu(T)\quad\quad\text{~for $i\in E$}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mi>T</mi></munder><mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.448em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mi>T</mi></munder><mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>∋</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mrow><mtext> for </mtext><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Big(\sum_{T}\mu(T)e_{T}\Big)_{i}\,=\,\sum_{T}\mu(T)(e_{T})_{i}\,=\,\sum_{T\ni i}\mu(T)\quad\quad\text{~for $i\in E$}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p10.2" class="ltx_p">With this translation, statements 1., 2. and 3. are equivalent by definition of non-separable and positive distributions. By the inclusion positive <math id="S2.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\supset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⊃</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\supset</annotation></semantics></math> log-linear we have <math id="S2.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4.\Rightarrow 3." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo stretchy="false">⇒</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4.\Rightarrow 3.</annotation></semantics></math> and by definition of relative resistances, we have <math id="S2.p10.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5.\Leftrightarrow 4." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo stretchy="false">⇔</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5.\Leftrightarrow 4.</annotation></semantics></math> It remains to prove <math id="S2.p10.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3.\Rightarrow 4." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo stretchy="false">⇒</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3.\Rightarrow 4.</annotation></semantics></math> and the correspondence of points in <math id="S2.p10.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(G)^{\circ}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(G)^{\circ}</annotation></semantics></math> with unique weights.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p11.1" class="ltx_p">Birch’s theorem in statistics <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>, Cor. 8.25]</cite> says that there is a unique log-linear distribution <math id="S2.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{c}</annotation></semantics></math> among all positive distributions with the same edge marginals. This is precisely <math id="S2.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3.\Rightarrow 4." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo stretchy="false">⇒</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3.\Rightarrow 4.</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p12.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, the only way in which different choices of edge weights <math id="S2.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c,\tilde{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>c</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c,\tilde{c}</annotation></semantics></math> lead to the same log-linear distribution <math id="S2.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{c}=\mu_{\tilde{c}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>c</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{c}=\mu_{\tilde{c}}</annotation></semantics></math> is if they differ by a multiplicative factor in each biconnected component. This follows from the fact that all spanning trees <math id="S2.p12.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> contain equally many edges from each of the biconnected components, and that these are the only edge subsets with this property. Thus, by uniqueness from Birch’s theorem, every point <math id="S2.p12.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\in P(G)^{\circ}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\in P(G)^{\circ}</annotation></semantics></math> corresponds to a unique choice of finite positive edge weights <math id="S2.p12.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math>, up to rescaling in the biconnected components of <math id="S2.p12.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem10" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem10.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 2.10</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem10.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem10.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem10.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem10.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Following Theorem <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem9" title="Theorem 2.9. ‣ 2 Relative resistance &amp; the spanning tree polytope ‣ Graphs with nonnegative resistance curvature" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.9</span></a>, relative resistances <math id="S2.Thmtheorem10.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\in P(G)^{\circ}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\in P(G)^{\circ}</annotation></semantics></math> can be used as an alternative way to parametrize edge weights on graphs. The theory of moment maps in symplectic geometry <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite> and toric geometry <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>, Thm. 8.24]</cite> furthermore implies that the relation between these two parametrizations is a diffeomorphism. More precisely, the interior of <math id="S2.Thmtheorem10.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(G)</annotation></semantics></math> is diffeomorphic, under the inverse moment map, to the space of all <math id="S2.Thmtheorem10.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math>-weighted cut spaces of the graph, i.e., the <math id="S2.Thmtheorem10.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math>-scaled images of the coboundary operator <math id="S2.Thmtheorem10.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d^{*}_{G}:\mathbb{R}^{V}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mi>G</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>V</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>E</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d^{*}_{G}:\mathbb{R}^{V}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{E}</annotation></semantics></math>. These vector subspaces form the real open part of a toric subvariety of the Grassmannian. This is a well-known perspective for matroid theorists since <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>, but the interpretation of effective and relative resistances as images of the moment map seems to have gone unnoticed until recently in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem11" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem11.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 2.11</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem11.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(computation)<span id="S2.Thmtheorem11.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem11.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem11.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem11.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">In practice, the relation between edge weights and relative resistances is efficiently computable. Given <math id="S2.Thmtheorem11.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math>, one can compute the relative resistances <math id="S2.Thmtheorem11.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> as the diagonal of the projection matrix onto the <math id="S2.Thmtheorem11.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math>-weighted cut space <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>. The hardest step in this procedure is a <math id="S2.Thmtheorem11.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|V|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|V|</annotation></semantics></math>-sized matrix inversion. Alternatively, there are highly optimized approximation algorithms <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>. Given <math id="S2.Thmtheorem11.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>, one can compute the corresponding edge weights iteratively: pick a random point <math id="S2.Thmtheorem11.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{c}\in\mathbb{R}^{E}_{&gt;0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>c</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>E</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{c}\in\mathbb{R}^{E}_{&gt;0}</annotation></semantics></math> and compute <math id="S2.Thmtheorem11.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{r}(\hat{c})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>c</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{r}(\hat{c})</annotation></semantics></math>. Then repeat the steps below until <math id="S2.Thmtheorem11.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{r}\approx r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>≈</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{r}\approx r</annotation></semantics></math> as desired:</span></p>
<table id="S2.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><span id="S2.Ex6.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_italic">1. Let <math id="S2.Ex6.m1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{c}_{e}\,\leftarrow\,\hat{c}_{e}\cdot(r_{e}/\hat{r}_{e})^{1/(|V|-1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>c</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>e</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.448em" rspace="0.448em" stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>c</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>e</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{c}_{e}\,\leftarrow\,\hat{c}_{e}\cdot(r_{e}/\hat{r}_{e})^{1/(|V|-1)}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S2.Ex6.m1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e\in E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e\in E</annotation></semantics></math></span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.Ex7"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><span id="S2.Ex7.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_italic">2. Compute <math id="S2.Ex7.m1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{r}(\hat{c})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>c</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{r}(\hat{c})</annotation></semantics></math></span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem11.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem11.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The theory of operator scaling guarantees convergence of the iterates in polynomial time <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p13.1" class="ltx_p">We conclude this section by giving an explicit characterization of relative resistances based on the hyperplane description of <math id="S2.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(G)</annotation></semantics></math>. This greatly generalizes the bounds in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, Prop. 2.16-17]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem12" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem12.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 2.12</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem12.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem12.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem12.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem12.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S2.Thmtheorem12.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> be a graph. Then <math id="S2.Thmtheorem12.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\in\mathbb{R}^{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>E</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\in\mathbb{R}^{E}</annotation></semantics></math> are relative resistances in <math id="S2.Thmtheorem12.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if</span></p>
<table id="S2.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\leq\sum_{e\in A}r_{e}\leq|V|-\textup{\#components of $(G-E\backslash A)$}\quad\quad\textup{for all $A\subseteq E$}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo rspace="0.111em">≤</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mtext>#components of </mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">\</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mrow><mtext>for all </mtext><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\leq\sum_{e\in A}r_{e}\leq|V|-\textup{\#components of $(G-E\backslash A)$}\quad\quad\textup{for all $A\subseteq E$},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem12.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem12.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">with equality in the upper bound if and only if <math id="S2.Thmtheorem12.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> are the edges of an induced subgraph on some (possibly none or all) biconnected components of <math id="S2.Thmtheorem12.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, and equality in the lower bound if and only if <math id="S2.Thmtheorem12.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> is empty.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p14" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p14.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p14.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof.</span>
Expression (<a href="#S2.E3" title="In Corollary 2.12. ‣ 2 Relative resistance &amp; the spanning tree polytope ‣ Graphs with nonnegative resistance curvature" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>) are the hyperplane inequalities that cut out the spanning tree polytope <math id="S2.p14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(G)</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S2.p14.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>E</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{E}</annotation></semantics></math>; this follows for instance from the hyperplane description of matroid base polytopes in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>, Prop. 2.3]</cite>. By Foster’s local theorem, the upper bound in (<a href="#S2.E3" title="In Corollary 2.12. ‣ 2 Relative resistance &amp; the spanning tree polytope ‣ Graphs with nonnegative resistance curvature" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>) is an equality when <math id="S2.p14.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> are the edges of an induced subgraph on biconnected components of <math id="S2.p14.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>. This includes <math id="S2.p14.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A=E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A=E</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.p14.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A=\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A=\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math> where also the lower bound is attained and equal to zero.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p15" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p15.1" class="ltx_p">Since the codimension of <math id="S2.p15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(G)</annotation></semantics></math> is equal to the number of biconnected components of <math id="S2.p15.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> — the corresponding matroid polytope has codimension equal to the number of connected components of the matroid <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>, Prop. 2.4]</cite> —, no further independent equalities can hold on the relative interior of <math id="S2.p15.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(G)</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence all other inequalities in (<a href="#S2.E3" title="In Corollary 2.12. ‣ 2 Relative resistance &amp; the spanning tree polytope ‣ Graphs with nonnegative resistance curvature" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>) must be strict, which completes the proof.
∎</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p16" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p16.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, we note that the results above extend to weights which can be zero or infinite. This closure is well-understood from the perspective of toric geometry, and it completes the space of possible relative resistances to the full spanning tree polytope <math id="S2.p16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(G)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Resistance nonnegative graphs</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We return to the main topic of this article. Recall the definition of resistance curvature.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 3.1</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(resistance curvature)<span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The <em id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">resistance curvature</em> of a vertex <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in V</annotation></semantics></math> is</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{v}(c)=1-\frac{1}{2}\sum_{uv\in E}r_{uv}(c)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{v}(c)=1-\frac{1}{2}\sum_{uv\in E}r_{uv}(c).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We make the definitions introduced in Question <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem2" title="Question 1.2. ‣ 1.1 Motivation ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Graphs with nonnegative resistance curvature" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.2</span></a> explicit.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 3.2</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(RN, RP, SRN)<span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">A graph is <em id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">resistance nonnegative</em> (RN) if there exist weights <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;c&lt;\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;c&lt;\infty</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{v}(c)\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{v}(c)\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>. A graph is <em id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">resistance positive</em> (RP) if there exists weights such that <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{v}(c)&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{v}(c)&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>. A graph is <em id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">strictly resistance nonnegative</em> (SRN) if it is RN but not RP.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We start with some examples; the reader is invited to verify these using other techniques.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_example">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Example 3.3</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(RP graph)<span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is vertex-transitive, then for constant edge weights <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c=1</annotation></semantics></math> we find <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{v}(1)=|V|^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{v}(1)=|V|^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in V</annotation></semantics></math>. This is shown for instance in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>, Prop. 7]</cite>. As a result, every vertex-transitive graph is RP. In particular, cycle graphs and the Petersen graph are RP.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_example">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Example 3.4</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(SRN graph)<span id="S3.Thmtheorem4.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\in\mathbb{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\in\mathbb{N}</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G=K_{n,n+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G=K_{n,n+1}</annotation></semantics></math> be the near-balanced complete bipartite graph on vertex sets <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{n},V_{n+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{n},V_{n+1}</annotation></semantics></math>, and pick constant edge weights <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c=1</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is edge-transitive, <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{e}(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{e}(1)</annotation></semantics></math> is constant and by Foster’s theorem we find <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{v}(1)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{v}(1)=0</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in V_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in V_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{u}(1)=1/(n+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{u}(1)=1/(n+1)</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u\in V_{n+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u\in V_{n+1}</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{n,n+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{n,n+1}</annotation></semantics></math> is RN.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Then, for any choice of edge weights <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math>, we use Foster’s theorem to find that</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{v\in V_{n}}p_{v}(c)=n-\tfrac{1}{2}\sum_{v\in V_{n}}\sum_{u\in V_{n+1}}r_{uv}(c)=n-\tfrac{1}{2}\sum_{e\in E}r_{e}(c)\overset{\text{(Foster)}}{=}n-\tfrac{1}{2}(2n+1-1)=0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></munder><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo>=</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic" mathsize="0.700em">(Foster)</mtext></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{v\in V_{n}}p_{v}(c)=n-\tfrac{1}{2}\sum_{v\in V_{n}}\sum_{u\in V_{n+1}}r_{uv}(c)=n-\tfrac{1}{2}\sum_{e\in E}r_{e}(c)\overset{\text{(Foster)}}{=}n-\tfrac{1}{2}(2n+1-1)=0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">So in particular we cannot have <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{v}(c)&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{v}(c)&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in V_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in V_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> and thus <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{n,n+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{n,n+1}</annotation></semantics></math> must be SRN. The same approach works in any bipartite graph, which leads to the following result:</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 3.5</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">A bipartite graph <math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is not RN if its bipartition sizes differ by more than one and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is not RP if its bipartition sizes differ.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_example">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Example 3.6</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(not RN graph)<span id="S3.Thmtheorem6.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> be a graph with cut vertex <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e., <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G-x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G-x</annotation></semantics></math> is disconnected. Following <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>, Prop. 2]</cite>, we then have that <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{x}(c)\leq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{x}(c)\leq 0</annotation></semantics></math> with equality if and only if <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> is an interior vertex in a path graph <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus the only non-biconnected RN graphs are paths, which are SRN.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 3.7</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If <math id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is RN and not biconnected then <math id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is a path, and if <math id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|V|\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|V|\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math> it is SRN.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We continue with a first characterization theorem that answers Question <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem2" title="Question 1.2. ‣ 1.1 Motivation ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Graphs with nonnegative resistance curvature" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem8" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3.8</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">A graph <math id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is RN (resp. RP) if and only if there exists a <math id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{</annotation></semantics></math>non-separable, log-linear, positive<math id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">}</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\}</annotation></semantics></math> distribution on <math id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}(G)</annotation></semantics></math>, such that the expected degree of every vertex in a random spanning tree under this distribution is at most <math id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> (resp. strictly smaller than <math id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>). Any type of distribution is sufficient and all three types are necessary.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof.</span>
The expected degree of a vertex <math id="S3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> in a random spanning tree with distribution <math id="S3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math> is</p>
<table id="S3.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{T}\mu(T)\sum_{uv\in T}1\,=\,\sum_{uv\in E}\Big(\sum_{T\ni uv}\mu(T)\Big)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mi>T</mi></munder><mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></munder><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.448em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>∋</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></mrow></munder><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{T}\mu(T)\sum_{uv\in T}1\,=\,\sum_{uv\in E}\Big(\sum_{T\ni uv}\mu(T)\Big).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p5.2" class="ltx_p">In other words, the expected degree is given in terms of the edge marginals of <math id="S3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S3.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>μ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> be a non-separable distribution for which the expected degree of each vertex is at most <math id="S3.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>. By Theorem <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem9" title="Theorem 2.9. ‣ 2 Relative resistance &amp; the spanning tree polytope ‣ Graphs with nonnegative resistance curvature" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.9</span></a>, the edge marginals of <math id="S3.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>μ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> are equal to relative resistances for some choice of edge weights <math id="S3.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> and thus we find for every <math id="S3.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in V</annotation></semantics></math> that</p>
<table id="S3.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\geq\sum_{uv\in E}\Big(\sum_{T\ni uv}\mu^{*}(T)\Big)=\sum_{uv\in E}r_{uv}(c^{*})=2-2p_{v}(c^{*})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo rspace="0.111em">≥</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>∋</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></mrow></munder><mrow><msup><mi>μ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\geq\sum_{uv\in E}\Big(\sum_{T\ni uv}\mu^{*}(T)\Big)=\sum_{uv\in E}r_{uv}(c^{*})=2-2p_{v}(c^{*}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p5.3" class="ltx_p">which shows that <math id="S3.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(c^{*})\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(c^{*})\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> and thus that <math id="S3.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is RN. Since non-separable distributions are the most general type, sufficiency of all three types follows. The converse statement and the strict version for RP graphs are proven in the same way.
∎</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p6.1" class="ltx_p">We now turn to a geometric version of this characterization. As the proof above illustrates, the expected degree constraints are affine inequalities on <math id="S3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>E</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{E}</annotation></semantics></math>. We define the (open) halfspaces</p>
<table id="S3.Ex12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{v}=\Big\{x\in\mathbb{R}^{E}\mid\sum_{uv\in E}x_{uv}\leq 2\Big\},\quad{\mathcal{H}_{v}}^{\circ}=\Big\{x\in\mathbb{R}^{E}\mid\sum_{uv\in E}x_{uv}&lt;2\Big\}\quad\quad\text{for~}v\in V." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>E</mi></msup></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>v</mi><mrow></mrow><mrow></mrow><mo>∘</mo></mmultiscripts><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>E</mi></msup></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mtext>for </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{v}=\Big\{x\in\mathbb{R}^{E}\mid\sum_{uv\in E}x_{uv}\leq 2\Big\},\quad{\mathcal{H}_{v}}^{\circ}=\Big\{x\in\mathbb{R}^{E}\mid\sum_{uv\in E}x_{uv}&lt;2\Big\}\quad\quad\text{for~}v\in V.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem9" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem9.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 3.9</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem9.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem9.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem9.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">A graph <math id="S3.Thmtheorem9.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is RN (resp. RP) if and only <math id="S3.Thmtheorem9.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(G)^{\circ}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(G)^{\circ}</annotation></semantics></math> and the halfspaces <math id="S3.Thmtheorem9.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> (resp. open halfspaces <math id="S3.Thmtheorem9.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{v}^{\circ}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>v</mi><mo>∘</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{v}^{\circ}</annotation></semantics></math>) for all <math id="S3.Thmtheorem9.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in V</annotation></semantics></math> have a common intersection point.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof.</span>
As in the proof of Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem8" title="Theorem 3.8. ‣ 3 Resistance nonnegative graphs ‣ Graphs with nonnegative resistance curvature" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.8</span></a>, a graph is RN if and only if it has relative resistances that satisfy <math id="S3.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{uv\in E}r_{uv}\leq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{uv\in E}r_{uv}\leq 2</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S3.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in V</annotation></semantics></math>, equivalently, if <math id="S3.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\in\mathcal{H}_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\in\mathcal{H}_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S3.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="S3.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(G)^{\circ}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(G)^{\circ}</annotation></semantics></math> is the set of all possible relative resistances, this completes the proof; the same proof holds for RP.
∎</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem10" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem10.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 3.10</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem10.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(computation)<span id="S3.Thmtheorem10.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem10.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem10.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem10.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Corollary <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem9" title="Corollary 3.9. ‣ 3 Resistance nonnegative graphs ‣ Graphs with nonnegative resistance curvature" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.9</span></a> shows that determining if a graph is RN can be done using linear programming. In practice, this requires some tolerance <math id="S3.Thmtheorem10.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math> to turn strict inequalities into non-strict ones. Since spanning tree polytopes can have exponentially many (in <math id="S3.Thmtheorem10.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|E|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|E|</annotation></semantics></math>) defining inequalities, solving the linear program may not be efficient. Is there a better algorithm?</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem11" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_question">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem11.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Question 3.11</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem11.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem11.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem11.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem11.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">What is the computational complexity of the decision problems:</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><span id="S3.Ex13.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_italic"><em id="S3.Ex13.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">For a given graph <math id="S3.Ex13.m1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, decide if <math id="S3.Ex13.m1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is RN/RP/SRN.</em></span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Trees &amp; double matchings</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We continue with the geometric characterization of RN and RP graphs in Corollary <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem9" title="Corollary 3.9. ‣ 3 Resistance nonnegative graphs ‣ Graphs with nonnegative resistance curvature" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.9</span></a>, and show that the intersection of <math id="S4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(G)</annotation></semantics></math> with the halfspaces <math id="S4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{H}_{v})_{v\in V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{H}_{v})_{v\in V}</annotation></semantics></math> has a combinatorial interpretation in terms of double matchings. Recall that a matching is a (possibly empty) subset of edges <math id="S4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M\subseteq E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M\subseteq E</annotation></semantics></math> which covers every vertex at most once, and the matching polytope <math id="S4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M(G)</annotation></semantics></math> is the convex hull of indicator vectors of matchings. Edmonds derived the hyperplane description of the matching polytope in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>; scaled by a factor of two, this becomes:</p>
<table id="S4.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2M(G)=\left\{x\in\mathbb{R}^{E}_{\geq 0}\,\,\bigg|\,\,\begin{array}[]{l}\sum_{uv\in E}x_{uv}\leq 2\quad\quad\quad\quad\quad\text{~for all~}v\in V,\\
\sum_{uv\in E(U)}x_{uv}\leq 2\lfloor\tfrac{1}{2}|U|\rfloor\quad\text{~for all odd-sized~}U\subseteq V\end{array}\right\}\subset\mathbb{R}^{E}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>E</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" mathsize="2.100em" rspace="0.507em">|</mo><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub></mstyle><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mspace style="width:5em;" width="5em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mtext> for all </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mstyle><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mtext> for all odd-sized </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>}</mo></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>E</mi></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2M(G)=\left\{x\in\mathbb{R}^{E}_{\geq 0}\,\,\bigg|\,\,\begin{array}[]{l}\sum_{uv\in E}x_{uv}\leq 2\quad\quad\quad\quad\quad\text{~for all~}v\in V,\\
\sum_{uv\in E(U)}x_{uv}\leq 2\lfloor\tfrac{1}{2}|U|\rfloor\quad\text{~for all odd-sized~}U\subseteq V\end{array}\right\}\subset\mathbb{R}^{E},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(U)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(U)</annotation></semantics></math> are the edges in the induced subgraph on <math id="S4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math>. Note that the <math id="S4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math>-based inequalities correspond to the expected degree condition. We show that the <math id="S4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math>-based inequalities are implied by the hyperplanes of <math id="S4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(G)</annotation></semantics></math>, which leads to the following characterization of RN graphs:</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 4.1</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">A graph <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is RN if and only if <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(G)^{\circ}\cap 2M(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(G)^{\circ}\cap 2M(G)</annotation></semantics></math> is non-empty.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof.</span>
If a point <math id="S4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\in P(G)^{\circ}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\in P(G)^{\circ}</annotation></semantics></math> lies in <math id="S4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2M(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2M(G)</annotation></semantics></math>, then by the <math id="S4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math>-based inequalities of <math id="S4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2M(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2M(G)</annotation></semantics></math> we know that r lies in the intersection of <math id="S4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{H}_{v})_{v\in V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{H}_{v})_{v\in V}</annotation></semantics></math> and thus that <math id="S4.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is RN by Corollary <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem9" title="Corollary 3.9. ‣ 3 Resistance nonnegative graphs ‣ Graphs with nonnegative resistance curvature" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.9</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is RN then there is a point <math id="S4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\in P(G)^{\circ}\subset\mathbb{R}^{E}_{\geq 0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><msubsup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>E</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\in P(G)^{\circ}\subset\mathbb{R}^{E}_{\geq 0}</annotation></semantics></math> which satisfies the <math id="S4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math>-based inequalities of <math id="S4.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2M(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2M(G)</annotation></semantics></math>. It remains to show that <math id="S4.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> also satisfies the <math id="S4.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math>-based inequalities. Let <math id="S4.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\subseteq V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\subseteq V</annotation></semantics></math> have odd size and <math id="S4.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G[U]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G[U]</annotation></semantics></math> be the induced subgraph on <math id="S4.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> with edges <math id="S4.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(U)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(U)</annotation></semantics></math>. We have</p>
<table id="S4.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex14"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\sum_{e\in E(U)}r_{e}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder></mstyle><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\sum_{e\in E(U)}r_{e}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex14.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\leq|V|-\textup{\#component of $\big(G-E\setminus E(U)\big)$}\quad\text{(Corollary \ref{cor: r hyperplane inequalities})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">≤</mo><mo fence="false" lspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mtext>#component of </mtext><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mtext>(Corollary </mtext><mtext><a href="#S2.Thmtheorem12" title="Corollary 2.12. ‣ 2 Relative resistance &amp; the spanning tree polytope ‣ Graphs with nonnegative resistance curvature" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.12</span></a></mtext><mtext>)</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leq|V|-\textup{\#component of $\big(G-E\setminus E(U)\big)$}\quad\text{(Corollary \ref{cor: r hyperplane inequalities})}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex15"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=|U|-\text{\#components of $\big(G[U]\big)$}\,\leq\,|U|-1\,=\,2\lfloor\tfrac{1}{2}|U|\rfloor." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mtext>#components of </mtext><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.448em">≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0.448em">=</mo><mrow><mn> 2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=|U|-\text{\#components of $\big(G[U]\big)$}\,\leq\,|U|-1\,=\,2\lfloor\tfrac{1}{2}|U|\rfloor.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p3.2" class="ltx_p">This confirms that <math id="S4.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\in 2M(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\in 2M(G)</annotation></semantics></math> and completes the proof.
∎</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 4.2</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Similar to how <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(G)^{\circ}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(G)^{\circ}</annotation></semantics></math> parametrizes all possible edge weights, the set <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(G)^{\circ}\cap 2M(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(G)^{\circ}\cap 2M(G)</annotation></semantics></math> parametrizes all edge weights for which <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> has nonnegative resistance curvature. Geometrically, thinking of edge weights as inverse edge lengths, this is the “space of metrics with nonnegative curvature on <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>”. The fact that the closure of this set is a polytope is in stark contrast with the complexity of the “space of metrics with positive/nonnegative curvature on a manifold” as studied in differential geometry; see for instance <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>, Sec. 2]</cite>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">A matching in a graph can have size at most <math id="S4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lfloor\tfrac{1}{2}|V|\rfloor" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lfloor\tfrac{1}{2}|V|\rfloor</annotation></semantics></math>. Such a matching is called <em id="S4.p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">perfect</em> if the graph is even, and <em id="S4.p4.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">near-perfect</em> if the graph is odd. Perfect matchings in graphs and their statistics are very actively studied; they are also called <em id="S4.p4.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">dimers</em>. It follows from <math id="S4.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>-tough that RP graphs have a (near-)perfect matching, but we can strengthen this result to all RN graphs.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 4.3</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Every resistance nonnegative graph has a (near-)perfect matching.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof.</span>
By Foster’s theorem, the spanning tree polytope lies in the hyperplane of points in <math id="S4.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>E</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{E}</annotation></semantics></math> whose entries sum to <math id="S4.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|V|-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|V|-1</annotation></semantics></math>. If a graph <math id="S4.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is RN, then by Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 4.1. ‣ 4 Trees &amp; double matchings ‣ Graphs with nonnegative resistance curvature" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a> there must be a point in <math id="S4.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2M(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2M(G)</annotation></semantics></math> that lies in this hyperplane. Since this point is a convex combination of indicator vectors of double matchings, <math id="S4.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> must have a matching of size at least <math id="S4.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lceil\tfrac{1}{2}(|V|-1)\rceil=\lfloor\tfrac{1}{2}|V|\rfloor" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌈</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌉</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lceil\tfrac{1}{2}(|V|-1)\rceil=\lfloor\tfrac{1}{2}|V|\rfloor</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p6.1" class="ltx_p">We note that Corollary <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem3" title="Corollary 4.3. ‣ 4 Trees &amp; double matchings ‣ Graphs with nonnegative resistance curvature" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a> is useful to check if a class of graphs could be RN. It can be used for instance to show that imbalanced bipartite graphs are not RN, as in Proposition <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem5" title="Proposition 3.5. ‣ 3 Resistance nonnegative graphs ‣ Graphs with nonnegative resistance curvature" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.5</span></a>.

<br class="ltx_break"> 
<br class="ltx_break">Motivated by Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 4.1. ‣ 4 Trees &amp; double matchings ‣ Graphs with nonnegative resistance curvature" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a> and Remark <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem2" title="Remark 4.2. ‣ 4 Trees &amp; double matchings ‣ Graphs with nonnegative resistance curvature" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>, we take a better look at the following polytope:</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 4.4</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The <em id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">tree double matching</em> (TDM) polytope of a graph is <math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta(G)=P(G)\cap 2M(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta(G)=P(G)\cap 2M(G)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_example">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Example 4.5</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(even cycle graphs)<span id="S4.Thmtheorem5.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For <math id="S4.Thmtheorem5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=2k\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=2k\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math> even, the cycle graph <math id="S4.Thmtheorem5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> has <math id="S4.Thmtheorem5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> spanning trees, <math id="S4.Thmtheorem5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> perfect matchings of size <math id="S4.Thmtheorem5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.Thmtheorem5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> matchings of size <math id="S4.Thmtheorem5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k-1</annotation></semantics></math>. Each of the spanning trees can be composed by combining a <math id="S4.Thmtheorem5.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(k-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(k-1)</annotation></semantics></math>-sized matching and one of the two <math id="S4.Thmtheorem5.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-sized matching. The TDM-polytope is the whole spanning tree polytope <math id="S4.Thmtheorem5.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta(C_{n})=P(C_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta(C_{n})=P(C_{n})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Integer points of polytopes are important in many areas of combinatorics and its applications; a polytope is called a <em id="S4.p7.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">lattice polytope</em> if its vertices are integer points. For the TDM-polytope, we find that the integer points <math id="S4.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta(G)\cap\mathbb{Z}^{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>E</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta(G)\cap\mathbb{Z}^{E}</annotation></semantics></math> have a simple characterization; recall that a <em id="S4.p7.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Hamiltonian path</em> in a graph is a spanning tree which is also a path.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 4.6</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The integer points of <math id="S4.Thmtheorem6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta(G)</annotation></semantics></math> are indicator vectors of Hamiltonian paths.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof.</span>
Since the vertices of <math id="S4.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(G)</annotation></semantics></math> lie in <math id="S4.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{0,1\}^{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mi>E</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{0,1\}^{E}</annotation></semantics></math> the only integer points in <math id="S4.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(G)</annotation></semantics></math> are its vertices, which are indicator vectors of spanning trees. A vertex <math id="S4.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S4.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(G)</annotation></semantics></math> lies in <math id="S4.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta(G)</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if it can be written as a convex combination of double matchings in <math id="S4.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>. By Foster’s theorem as in the proof of Corollary <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem3" title="Corollary 4.3. ‣ 4 Trees &amp; double matchings ‣ Graphs with nonnegative resistance curvature" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a> and integrality of the point <math id="S4.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{T}</annotation></semantics></math>, this point must be a combination of (i) two matchings with <math id="S4.p8.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tfrac{1}{2}(|V|-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tfrac{1}{2}(|V|-1)</annotation></semantics></math> edges if <math id="S4.p8.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is odd, or (ii) one matching with <math id="S4.p8.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tfrac{1}{2}|V|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tfrac{1}{2}|V|</annotation></semantics></math> edges and another one with <math id="S4.p8.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tfrac{1}{2}|V|-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tfrac{1}{2}|V|-1</annotation></semantics></math> edges if <math id="S4.p8.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is even. Combining these matchings gives a subgraph with degree at most <math id="S4.p8.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>, which is connected if and only if it is a path. This completes the proof.
∎</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p9.1" class="ltx_p">We can use this result to give a combinatorial condition for the RN property; a set of Hamiltonian paths <math id="S4.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> is called <em id="S4.p9.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">independent</em> if the indicator vectors <math id="S4.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(e_{T})_{T\in\mathcal{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(e_{T})_{T\in\mathcal{F}}</annotation></semantics></math> are affinely independent.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 4.7</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If <math id="S4.Thmtheorem7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> contains <math id="S4.Thmtheorem7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(|E|-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">−</mo></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">𝐶𝐿𝑂𝑆𝐸</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(|E|-</annotation></semantics></math>#biconnected components of <math id="S4.Thmtheorem7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">𝑂𝑃𝐸𝑁</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G)</annotation></semantics></math> independent Hamiltonian paths, then <math id="S4.Thmtheorem7.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is RN.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p10" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof.</span>
The result is true if <math id="S4.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is a path graph so we may assume that <math id="S4.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is biconnected. If <math id="S4.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> contains <math id="S4.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|E|-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|E|-1</annotation></semantics></math> independent Hamiltonian paths, then <math id="S4.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{conv}(e_{T}\mid T\text{~is Hamiltonian path})\subseteq\Theta(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>conv</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> is Hamiltonian path</mtext></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{conv}(e_{T}\mid T\text{~is Hamiltonian path})\subseteq\Theta(G)</annotation></semantics></math> has dimension <math id="S4.p10.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|E|-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|E|-1</annotation></semantics></math>. Since this is equal to the dimension of <math id="S4.p10.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(G)</annotation></semantics></math>, this convex hull and thus also <math id="S4.p10.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta(G)</annotation></semantics></math> must intersect <math id="S4.p10.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(G)^{\circ}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(G)^{\circ}</annotation></semantics></math>. By Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 4.1. ‣ 4 Trees &amp; double matchings ‣ Graphs with nonnegative resistance curvature" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a> this completes the proof.
∎</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p11.1" class="ltx_p">Proposition <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem6" title="Proposition 4.6. ‣ 4 Trees &amp; double matchings ‣ Graphs with nonnegative resistance curvature" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.6</span></a> and its proof illustrate how the integer points of the TDM polytope correspond combinatorially to trees that can be constructed as the union of two matchings. A similar combinatorial interpretation holds for rational points of <math id="S4.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta(G)</annotation></semantics></math> or, equivalently, integer points of its dilations. We propose the following Ehrhart-type question for TDM polytopes:</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem8" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_question">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Question 4.8</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem8.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Count the number of integer points <math id="S4.Thmtheorem8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{k}=\#(k\Theta(G)\cap\mathbb{Z}^{E})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">#</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>E</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{k}=\#(k\Theta(G)\cap\mathbb{Z}^{E})</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S4.Thmtheorem8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\in\mathbb{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\in\mathbb{N}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p12.1" class="ltx_p">Let us return to Example <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem5" title="Example 4.5 (even cycle graphs). ‣ 4 Trees &amp; double matchings ‣ Graphs with nonnegative resistance curvature" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.5</span></a>, where we mentioned that <math id="S4.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta(G)</annotation></semantics></math> coincides with <math id="S4.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(G)</annotation></semantics></math> for cycle graphs. It turns out that this is the only class of graphs where this occurs.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem9" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem9.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 4.9</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem9.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem9.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem9.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The following are equivalent for a biconnected graph <math id="S4.Thmtheorem9.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>:</span></p>
<ol id="S4.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S4.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta(G)=P(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta(G)=P(G)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">;</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(c)&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(c)&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> for all weights </span><math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;c&lt;\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;c&lt;\infty</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">;</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S4.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is a cycle or a single edge </span><math id="S4.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G=K_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G=K_{2}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p13" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p13.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p13.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof.</span>
Note that <math id="S4.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta(G)=P(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta(G)=P(G)</annotation></semantics></math> is equivalent to <math id="S4.p13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(G)^{\circ}\cap 2M(G)=P(G)^{\circ}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(G)^{\circ}\cap 2M(G)=P(G)^{\circ}</annotation></semantics></math>, which says that every point <math id="S4.p13.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\in P(G)^{\circ}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\in P(G)^{\circ}</annotation></semantics></math> lies in the halfspaces <math id="S4.p13.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{H}_{v})_{v\in V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{H}_{v})_{v\in V}</annotation></semantics></math>. This establishes <math id="S4.p13.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1.\Leftrightarrow 2." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo stretchy="false">⇔</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.\Leftrightarrow 2.</annotation></semantics></math> The implication <math id="S4.p13.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3.\Rightarrow 1." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo stretchy="false">⇒</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3.\Rightarrow 1.</annotation></semantics></math> follows from Proposition <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem6" title="Proposition 4.6. ‣ 4 Trees &amp; double matchings ‣ Graphs with nonnegative resistance curvature" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.6</span></a>. Conversely, if <math id="S4.p13.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta(G)=P(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta(G)=P(G)</annotation></semantics></math> then all vertices of <math id="S4.p13.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(G)</annotation></semantics></math> must be Hamiltonian paths, so all spanning trees of <math id="S4.p13.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> are paths. For a biconnected graph, this occurs if and only if <math id="S4.p13.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is a cycle or a single edge, which proves <math id="S4.p13.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1.\Rightarrow 3." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo stretchy="false">⇒</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.\Rightarrow 3.</annotation></semantics></math>
∎</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p14.1" class="ltx_p">We note that paths with more than <math id="S4.p14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math> vertices satisfy similar properties: <math id="S4.p14.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta(G)=P(G)=e_{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>G</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta(G)=P(G)=e_{G}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p14.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(c)\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(c)\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> for all weights. We propose to study the vertices of general TDM-polytopes:</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem10" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_question">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem10.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Question 4.10</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem10.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem10.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem10.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem10.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">What are the vertices of <math id="S4.Thmtheorem10.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta(G)</annotation></semantics></math>? What is the smallest <math id="S4.Thmtheorem10.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> such that the <math id="S4.Thmtheorem10.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-dilated TDM polytope is a lattice polytope, i.e., determine <math id="S4.Thmtheorem10.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{G}=\min\{k\in\mathbb{N}\mid k\Theta(G)\text{~is lattice polytope}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"> is lattice polytope</mtext></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{G}=\min\{k\in\mathbb{N}\mid k\Theta(G)\text{~is lattice polytope}\}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p15" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p15.1" class="ltx_p">More generally, the points in <math id="S4.p15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta(G)</annotation></semantics></math> are edge marginals of distributions <math id="S4.p15.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math> on spanning trees in <math id="S4.p15.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> and distributions <math id="S4.p15.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> on matchings in <math id="S4.p15.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, at the same time. In both cases, these distributions can be taken log-linear <math id="S4.p15.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{c}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p15.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{\tilde{c}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>c</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{\tilde{c}}</annotation></semantics></math> induced by some weights <math id="S4.p15.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p15.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>c</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{c}</annotation></semantics></math> on the edges.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem11" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_question">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem11.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Question 4.11</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem11.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem11.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem11.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem11.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">What is the relation between the edge weights <math id="S4.Thmtheorem11.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.Thmtheorem11.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>c</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{c}</annotation></semantics></math>, for which the distributions <math id="S4.Thmtheorem11.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{c}</annotation></semantics></math> on spanning trees and <math id="S4.Thmtheorem11.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{\tilde{c}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>c</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{\tilde{c}}</annotation></semantics></math> on matchings have the same edge marginals <math id="S4.Thmtheorem11.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in\Theta(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in\Theta(G)</annotation></semantics></math>?</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Algebraic characterization</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Until here, we have used the relation between effective resistances and spanning trees to characterize RN and RP graphs. However, the effective resistance has a rich algebraic theory independent of the combinatorics of spanning trees and matroids; in this section, we use this theory to derive one further characterization. More precisely, we start from an alternative parametrization for the space of all possible effective resistances in a graph — a space <math id="S5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{K}(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{K}(G)</annotation></semantics></math> with a similar role to <math id="S5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(G)^{\circ}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(G)^{\circ}</annotation></semantics></math> — and derive an analogue of Corollary <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem9" title="Corollary 3.9. ‣ 3 Resistance nonnegative graphs ‣ Graphs with nonnegative resistance curvature" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.9</span></a> to characterize RN and RP graphs. Interestingly, the condition <math id="S5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> again translates to affine inequalities on <math id="S5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{K}(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{K}(G)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We give a minimal treatment of Laplacian and resistance matrices and refer to <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, Ch. 3]</cite> for more details. Let <math id="S5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> be a connected graph with <math id="S5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=|V|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=|V|</annotation></semantics></math> vertices and edge weights <math id="S5.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;c&lt;\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;c&lt;\infty</annotation></semantics></math>. The Laplacian matrix of <math id="S5.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is the <math id="S5.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\times n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\times n</annotation></semantics></math> symmetric matrix <math id="S5.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> with entries</p>
<table id="S5.Ex16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex16.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="L_{uv}=0\text{~for $uv\not\in E$},\quad\quad L_{uv}=-c_{uv}\text{~for $uv\in E$},\quad\quad L_{vv}=-\sum_{u\in V}L_{uv}\text{~for $v\in V$}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mrow><mtext> for </mtext><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo>∉</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mi>L</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>c</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mtext> for </mtext><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mi>L</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.055em">−</mo><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msub><mi>L</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mtext> for </mtext><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{uv}=0\text{~for $uv\not\in E$},\quad\quad L_{uv}=-c_{uv}\text{~for $uv\in E$},\quad\quad L_{vv}=-\sum_{u\in V}L_{uv}\text{~for $v\in V$}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p2.2" class="ltx_p">This matrix is real, symmetric, positive semidefinite and has <math id="S5.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{ker}(L)=\operatorname{span}(\mathbf{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ker</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>span</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟏</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{ker}(L)=\operatorname{span}(\mathbf{1})</annotation></semantics></math> with all-ones vector <math id="S5.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{1}\in\mathbb{R}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>𝟏</mn><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{1}\in\mathbb{R}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>. Together with off-diagonal nonpositivity, these four properties characterize Laplacian matrices <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, Prop. 2.5]</cite>. Let <math id="S5.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{\dagger}=(L+\tfrac{1}{n}\mathbf{1}\mathbf{1}^{T})^{-1}-\tfrac{1}{n}\mathbf{11}^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mo>†</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mn>𝟏𝟏</mn><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mn>𝟏𝟏</mn><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{\dagger}=(L+\tfrac{1}{n}\mathbf{1}\mathbf{1}^{T})^{-1}-\tfrac{1}{n}\mathbf{11}^{T}</annotation></semantics></math> be the Moore–Penrose pseudoinverse of <math id="S5.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>, then the effective resistance between two vertices is defined as</p>
<table id="S5.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{uv}=L^{\dagger}_{uu}+L^{\dagger}_{vv}-2L^{\dagger}_{uv},\quad\quad\text{~for $u,v\in V$}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><mo>†</mo></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo>†</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo>†</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mtext> for </mtext><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{uv}=L^{\dagger}_{uu}+L^{\dagger}_{vv}-2L^{\dagger}_{uv},\quad\quad\text{~for $u,v\in V$}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p2.3" class="ltx_p">For adjacent vertices, this agrees with definition (<a href="#S1.E1" title="In 1.1 Motivation ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Graphs with nonnegative resistance curvature" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) in the introduction and we recall that <math id="S5.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{uv}=\omega_{uv}c_{uv}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{uv}=\omega_{uv}c_{uv}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S5.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="uv\in E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">uv\in E</annotation></semantics></math>. The <em id="S5.p2.3.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">resistance matrix</em> of <math id="S5.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is the <math id="S5.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\times n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\times n</annotation></semantics></math> matrix <math id="S5.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega=(\omega_{uv})_{u,v\in V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega=(\omega_{uv})_{u,v\in V}</annotation></semantics></math>. It is a real, symmetric, invertible matrix with zero diagonal. We will further work with the inverse resistance matrix <math id="S5.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=\Omega^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=\Omega^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math> and consider a special class of edge weights:</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 5.1</span></span><span id="S5.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The edge weights <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;c&lt;\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;c&lt;\infty</annotation></semantics></math> are called <em id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">normalized</em> if <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{u,v\in V}K_{uv}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{u,v\in V}K_{uv}=1</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We call a graph with normalized weights a <em id="S5.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">normalized graph</em>. Assuming normalized edge weights is not restrictive, since any choice of edge weights can be normalized by the common rescaling <math id="S5.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{e}\mapsto c_{e}/(\sum_{u,v\in V}K_{uv})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{e}\mapsto c_{e}/(\sum_{u,v\in V}K_{uv})</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S5.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e\in E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e\in E</annotation></semantics></math>. The resistance curvature of a normalized graph has a very simple expression in terms of <math id="S5.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 5.2</span></span><span id="S5.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, Cor. 3.10]</cite>)<span id="S5.Thmtheorem2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> be a normalized graph, then <math id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{v}(c)=\sum_{u\in V}K_{uv}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{v}(c)=\sum_{u\in V}K_{uv}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We now rephrase <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, Thm. 3.9]</cite> and characterize all possible inverse resistance matrices associated to a given graph as a semialgebraic set inside <math id="S5.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{GL}_{n}^{\text{sym}}(\mathbb{R})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>GL</mi><mi>n</mi><mtext>sym</mtext></msubsup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{GL}_{n}^{\text{sym}}(\mathbb{R})</annotation></semantics></math>, the set of real symmetric invertible <math id="S5.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\times n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\times n</annotation></semantics></math> matrices. We think of this as an alternative parametrization to <math id="S5.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(G)^{\circ}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(G)^{\circ}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 5.3</span></span><span id="S5.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>)<span id="S5.Thmtheorem3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The set of inverse resistance matrices of <math id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> with normalized weights is</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{K}(G)=\left\{K\in\operatorname{GL}_{n}^{\text{sym}}(\mathbb{R})\,\Biggl|\,\begin{array}[]{l}\sum_{u,v\in V}K_{uv}=1\\
K_{uv}=\big(\sum_{w\in V}K_{wv}\big)\big(\sum_{w\in V}K_{uw}\big)\quad\text{~for all $uv\not\in E$}\\
K_{uv}&gt;\big(\sum_{w\in V}K_{wv}\big)\big(\sum_{w\in V}K_{uw}\big)\quad\text{~for all $uv\in E$}\end{array}\right\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>GL</mi><mi>n</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">sym</mtext></msubsup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" mathsize="2.600em" rspace="0.337em">|</mo><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msub></mstyle><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msub></mstyle><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msub></mstyle><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"> for all </mtext><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo>∉</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msub></mstyle><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msub></mstyle><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"> for all </mtext><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{K}(G)=\left\{K\in\operatorname{GL}_{n}^{\text{sym}}(\mathbb{R})\,\Biggl|\,\begin{array}[]{l}\sum_{u,v\in V}K_{uv}=1\\
K_{uv}=\big(\sum_{w\in V}K_{wv}\big)\big(\sum_{w\in V}K_{uw}\big)\quad\text{~for all $uv\not\in E$}\\
K_{uv}&gt;\big(\sum_{w\in V}K_{wv}\big)\big(\sum_{w\in V}K_{uw}\big)\quad\text{~for all $uv\in E$}\end{array}\right\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof.</span>
We summarize the proof of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, Thm. 3.9]</cite>. Given <math id="S5.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K\in\mathcal{K}(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K\in\mathcal{K}(G)</annotation></semantics></math>, we construct the matrix</p>
<table id="S5.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L=-2K+2(K\mathbf{1})(K\mathbf{1})^{T}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>𝟏</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>𝟏</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L=-2K+2(K\mathbf{1})(K\mathbf{1})^{T}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p5.2" class="ltx_p">It can be checked that this matrix is real, symmetric, positive semidefinite with <math id="S5.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{ker}(L)=\operatorname{span}(\mathbf{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ker</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>span</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟏</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{ker}(L)=\operatorname{span}(\mathbf{1})</annotation></semantics></math> and that it has nonpositive off-diagonal entries: <math id="S5.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{uv}&lt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{uv}&lt;0</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S5.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="uv\in E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">uv\in E</annotation></semantics></math> and zeros otherwise. Thus, <math id="S5.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> is a Laplacian matrix of <math id="S5.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> with weights <math id="S5.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c^{*}_{uv}=-L_{uv}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c^{*}_{uv}=-L_{uv}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S5.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> is the inverse resistance matrix of this graph by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, Cor. 3.8]</cite>. Here, <math id="S5.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K\in\mathcal{K}(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K\in\mathcal{K}(G)</annotation></semantics></math> confirms that the weights <math id="S5.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> are normalized.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Conversely, it can be checked that every inverse resistance matrix of <math id="S5.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> lies in the set <math id="S5.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{K}(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{K}(G)</annotation></semantics></math>; this follows for instance by relation (<a href="#S5.E6" title="In 5 Algebraic characterization ‣ Graphs with nonnegative resistance curvature" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>) between the Laplacian and inverse resistance matrix of a normalized graph, and then using the properties of Laplacian matrices.
∎</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p7.1" class="ltx_p">We now formulate an analogue of Corollary <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem9" title="Corollary 3.9. ‣ 3 Resistance nonnegative graphs ‣ Graphs with nonnegative resistance curvature" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.9</span></a> based on the parametrization <math id="S5.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{K}(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{K}(G)</annotation></semantics></math>. As before, we define (open) halfspaces to keep track of signs of the resistance curvature</p>
<table id="S5.Ex18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex18.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\widetilde{\mathcal{H}}_{v}=\Big\{K\in\operatorname{GL}_{n}^{\text{sym}}(\mathbb{R})\mid\sum_{u\in V}K_{uv}\geq 0\Big\},\quad\widetilde{\mathcal{H}}_{v}^{\circ}=\Big\{K\in\operatorname{GL}_{n}^{\text{sym}}(\mathbb{R})\mid\sum_{u\in V}K_{uv}&gt;0\Big\}\quad\text{~for $v\in V$}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi>GL</mi><mi>n</mi><mtext>sym</mtext></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>v</mi><mo>∘</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi>GL</mi><mi>n</mi><mtext>sym</mtext></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mtext> for </mtext><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathcal{H}}_{v}=\Big\{K\in\operatorname{GL}_{n}^{\text{sym}}(\mathbb{R})\mid\sum_{u\in V}K_{uv}\geq 0\Big\},\quad\widetilde{\mathcal{H}}_{v}^{\circ}=\Big\{K\in\operatorname{GL}_{n}^{\text{sym}}(\mathbb{R})\mid\sum_{u\in V}K_{uv}&gt;0\Big\}\quad\text{~for $v\in V$}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 5.4</span></span><span id="S5.Thmtheorem4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">A graph <math id="S5.Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is RN (resp. RP) if and only if <math id="S5.Thmtheorem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{K}(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{K}(G)</annotation></semantics></math> and the halfspaces <math id="S5.Thmtheorem4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\mathcal{H}}_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathcal{H}}_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> (resp. open halfspaces <math id="S5.Thmtheorem4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\mathcal{H}}_{v}^{\circ}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>v</mi><mo>∘</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathcal{H}}_{v}^{\circ}</annotation></semantics></math>) for all <math id="S5.Thmtheorem4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in V</annotation></semantics></math> have a common intersection point.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof.</span>
We may assume that all weights are normalized, without loss of generality. The corollary then immediately follows from the expression of <math id="S5.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> in terms of <math id="S5.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> in Proposition <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem2" title="Proposition 5.2 ([, Cor. 3.10]). ‣ 5 Algebraic characterization ‣ Graphs with nonnegative resistance curvature" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a>.
∎</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Graph operations</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We consider three graph operations and their effect on resistance nonnegativity and positivity.</p>
</div>
<section id="S6.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.1 </span>Subgraphs</h3>

<div id="S6.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Since adding edges to a graph increases its set of spanning trees, we obtain the following:</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 6.1</span></span><span id="S6.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S6.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H\subseteq G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H\subseteq G</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S6.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V(H)=V(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V(H)=V(G)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> connected. If <math id="S6.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> is RP then <math id="S6.Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is RP.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof.</span>
Let <math id="S6.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> be a connected RP subgraph of <math id="S6.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> on the same vertex set. The spanning tree sets of these two graphs satisfy <math id="S6.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}(H)\subseteq\mathcal{T}(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}(H)\subseteq\mathcal{T}(G)</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="S6.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> is RP, there exists a positive distribution <math id="S6.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S6.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}(H)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}(H)</annotation></semantics></math> whose edge marginals <math id="S6.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\in P(H)^{\circ}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\in P(H)^{\circ}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfy <math id="S6.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{uv\in E(H)}r_{uv}&lt;2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{uv\in E(H)}r_{uv}&lt;2</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S6.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in V</annotation></semantics></math>. We then define a distribution <math id="S6.SS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>μ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S6.SS1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}(G)</annotation></semantics></math> as</p>
<table id="S6.Ex19" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu^{*}:T\mapsto\begin{cases}(1-\varepsilon)\cdot\mu(T)\quad\quad\quad\quad\,\,\,\,\text{~if $T\in\mathcal{T}(H)$}\\
\varepsilon\cdot\big(|\mathcal{T}(G)\setminus\mathcal{T}(H)|\big)^{-1}\quad\text{~if $T\not\in\mathcal{T}(H)$},\end{cases}\quad\quad\text{~for $T\in\mathcal{T}(G)$.}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>μ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:4.68em;" width="4.68em"></mspace><mrow><mtext> if </mtext><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mtext> if </mtext><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>∉</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mrow><mtext> for </mtext><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu^{*}:T\mapsto\begin{cases}(1-\varepsilon)\cdot\mu(T)\quad\quad\quad\quad\,\,\,\,\text{~if $T\in\mathcal{T}(H)$}\\
\varepsilon\cdot\big(|\mathcal{T}(G)\setminus\mathcal{T}(H)|\big)^{-1}\quad\text{~if $T\not\in\mathcal{T}(H)$},\end{cases}\quad\quad\text{~for $T\in\mathcal{T}(G)$.}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">This is a positive distribution on <math id="S6.SS1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}(G)</annotation></semantics></math> with edge marginals <math id="S6.SS1.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r^{*}\in P(G)^{\circ}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r^{*}\in P(G)^{\circ}</annotation></semantics></math>. For sufficiently small <math id="S6.SS1.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> these marginals satisfy <math id="S6.SS1.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{uv\in E(G)}r^{*}_{uv}&lt;2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{uv\in E(G)}r^{*}_{uv}&lt;2</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S6.SS1.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math>, by linearity of <math id="S6.SS1.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r,r^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r,r^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> in terms of <math id="S6.SS1.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu,\mu^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>μ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu,\mu^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">A graph <math id="S6.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is <em id="S6.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Hamiltonian</em> if it contains a <math id="S6.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|V(G)|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|V(G)|</annotation></semantics></math>-length cycle graph as a subgraph.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 6.2</span></span><span id="S6.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Hamiltonian graphs are resistance positive, but the converse is not always true.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof.</span>
If <math id="S6.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is Hamiltonian, then it contains a cycle graph <math id="S6.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H\subseteq G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H\subseteq G</annotation></semantics></math> as a subgraph on the same vertex set. Since <math id="S6.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> is connected and RP, <math id="S6.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is also RP by Lemma <a href="#S6.Thmtheorem1" title="Lemma 6.1. ‣ 6.1 Subgraphs ‣ 6 Graph operations ‣ Graphs with nonnegative resistance curvature" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.1</span></a>. The Petersen graph is RP (since it is vertex-transitive) but not Hamiltonian, so RP does not imply Hamiltonicity.
∎</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">We note that the study of graph toughness and Hamiltonicity are closely related; see for instance <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>. Combining Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 1.3. ‣ 1.1 Motivation ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Graphs with nonnegative resistance curvature" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.3</span></a> and Theorem <a href="#S6.Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 6.2. ‣ 6.1 Subgraphs ‣ 6 Graph operations ‣ Graphs with nonnegative resistance curvature" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.2</span></a>, we find that RP graphs lie between these two graph classes: <em id="S6.SS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Hamiltonian <math id="S6.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\subset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⊂</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\subset</annotation></semantics></math> RP <math id="S6.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\subseteq" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⊆</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\subseteq</annotation></semantics></math> 1-tough</em>. How are they related more precisely?</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_question">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Question 6.3</span></span><span id="S6.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Does <math id="S6.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>-tough imply RP? Is every non-Hamiltonian 1-tough graph RP?</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">The first question was also posed by Fiedler in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>, p59]</cite>. More strongly, he stated that “we might conjecture” that the converse to <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>, Thm. 3.4.18]</cite> is true, in other words, that <math id="S6.SS1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>-tough implies RP.

<br class="ltx_break"> 
<br class="ltx_break">As a further application we consider grid graphs. The cartesian product <math id="S6.SS1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G\times H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G\times H</annotation></semantics></math> of two graphs has vertex set <math id="S6.SS1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V(G)\times V(H)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V(G)\times V(H)</annotation></semantics></math> and edge set <math id="S6.SS1.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{(u,x)(v,y)\mid uv\in E(G)\text{~or~}xy\in E(H)\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> or </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{(u,x)(v,y)\mid uv\in E(G)\text{~or~}xy\in E(H)\}</annotation></semantics></math>, where “or” is exclusive. A <em id="S6.SS1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">grid graph</em> is the cartesian product of two path graphs <math id="S6.SS1.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{n}\times P_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{n}\times P_{m}</annotation></semantics></math>. In <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite> it was shown that for constant edge weights <math id="S6.SS1.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c=1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.SS1.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m,n\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m,n\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math>, all grid graphs have negative resistance curvature on interior vertices and nonnegative resistance curvature on boundary vertices and if <math id="S6.SS1.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m&gt;3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m&gt;3</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S6.SS1.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n&gt;3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n&gt;3</annotation></semantics></math>, then the boundary vertices have positive curvature. Here we find that some of these grid graphs are RP. This illustrates the relevance of considering edge weights.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 6.4</span></span><span id="S6.Thmtheorem4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For <math id="S6.Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\cdot n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\cdot n</annotation></semantics></math> even and <math id="S6.Thmtheorem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m,n&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m,n&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math>, the grid graph <math id="S6.Thmtheorem4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{n}\times P_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{n}\times P_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> is RP.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS1.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS1.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS1.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof.</span>
For these parameters, <math id="S6.SS1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{n}\times P_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{n}\times P_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> is Hamiltonian and thus RP.
∎</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">We conjecture that all grid graphs are resistance nonnegative, and ask about generalizations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_conjecture">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Conjecture 6.5</span></span><span id="S6.Thmtheorem5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Grid graphs are RN)<span id="S6.Thmtheorem5.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmtheorem5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S6.Thmtheorem5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{n}\times P_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{n}\times P_{m}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.Thmtheorem5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is RN for all <math id="S6.Thmtheorem5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m,n\in\mathbb{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m,n\in\mathbb{N}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_question">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Question 6.6</span></span><span id="S6.Thmtheorem6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmtheorem6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Are all graphs <math id="S6.Thmtheorem6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{n}\times\dots\times P_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{n}\times\dots\times P_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> RN? Which biconnected planar graphs are RN?</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.2 </span>Kron reduction</h3>

<div id="S6.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Kron reduction is an important tool in electrical circuit analysis, which allows to reduce a large circuit to a smaller one while maintaining its electrical properties <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>. Combinatorially, this operation is very simple; we write <math id="S6.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U^{c}=V\setminus U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>U</mi><mi>c</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>∖</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U^{c}=V\setminus U</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial U=\{v\in U^{c}\mid uv\in E\text{~for some $u\in U$}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mphantom></mphantom><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>U</mi><mi>c</mi></msup></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mtext> for some </mtext><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial U=\{v\in U^{c}\mid uv\in E\text{~for some $u\in U$}\}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 6.7</span></span><span id="S6.Thmtheorem7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Kron reduction)<span id="S6.Thmtheorem7.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmtheorem7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S6.Thmtheorem7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> be a connected graph with vertex subset <math id="S6.Thmtheorem7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\subset V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\subset V</annotation></semantics></math>. The <em id="S6.Thmtheorem7.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">Kron reduction</em> of <math id="S6.Thmtheorem7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S6.Thmtheorem7.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is the graph obtained by removing <math id="S6.Thmtheorem7.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> and adding all possible edges between <math id="S6.Thmtheorem7.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial U</annotation></semantics></math>; we write <math id="S6.Thmtheorem7.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{kron}_{U}(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>kron</mi><mi>U</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{kron}_{U}(G)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem8" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 6.8</span></span><span id="S6.Thmtheorem8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(matched weights)<span id="S6.Thmtheorem8.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmtheorem8.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S6.Thmtheorem8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>G</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{G}</annotation></semantics></math> be the Kron reduction of <math id="S6.Thmtheorem8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in V</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S6.Thmtheorem8.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> with edge weights <math id="S6.Thmtheorem8.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math>. The <em id="S6.Thmtheorem8.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">matched weights</em> <math id="S6.Thmtheorem8.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>c</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{c}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S6.Thmtheorem8.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>G</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{G}</annotation></semantics></math> are defined as</span></p>
<table id="S6.Ex20" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{c}_{uv}=c_{uv}+\frac{c_{ux}\cdot c_{xv}}{\sum_{xy\in E(G)}c_{xy}}\text{~if $u,v\in\partial x$,}\quad\quad\quad\tilde{c}_{uv}=c_{uv}\text{~otherwise,}\quad\quad\quad\text{~for $uv\in E(\widetilde{G}).$}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>c</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"> if </mtext><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">∈</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">,</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:3em;" width="3em"></mspace><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>c</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"> otherwise,</mtext></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:3em;" width="3em"></mspace><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"> for </mtext><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>G</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{c}_{uv}=c_{uv}+\frac{c_{ux}\cdot c_{xv}}{\sum_{xy\in E(G)}c_{xy}}\text{~if $u,v\in\partial x$,}\quad\quad\quad\tilde{c}_{uv}=c_{uv}\text{~otherwise,}\quad\quad\quad\text{~for $uv\in E(\widetilde{G}).$}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We recall some relevant properties of Kron reductions; see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, Ch. 3]</cite>,<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite> for more details.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem9" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem9.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 6.9</span></span><span id="S6.Thmtheorem9.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(quotient property, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>)<span id="S6.Thmtheorem9.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmtheorem9.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem9.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S6.Thmtheorem9.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U=\{u_{1},\dots,u_{k}\}\subset V(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U=\{u_{1},\dots,u_{k}\}\subset V(G)</annotation></semantics></math> in arbitrary order. Then</span></p>
<table id="S6.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{kron}_{U}(G)=\operatorname{kron}_{u_{k}}\circ\dots\circ\operatorname{kron}_{u_{1}}(G)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>kron</mi><mi>U</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>kron</mi><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mrow><msub><mi>kron</mi><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{kron}_{U}(G)=\operatorname{kron}_{u_{k}}\circ\dots\circ\operatorname{kron}_{u_{1}}(G).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To understand Kron reduction it often suffices to understand Kron reduction of single vertices. For a general vertex subset <math id="S6.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\subset V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\subset V</annotation></semantics></math>, if the weights are matched at every step in the decomposition of <math id="S6.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{G}=\operatorname{kron}_{U}(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>G</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>kron</mi><mi>U</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{G}=\operatorname{kron}_{U}(G)</annotation></semantics></math> along single vertices as in (<a href="#S6.E7" title="In Lemma 6.9 (quotient property, []). ‣ 6.2 Kron reduction ‣ 6 Graph operations ‣ Graphs with nonnegative resistance curvature" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>), then we say that the final weights <math id="S6.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>c</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{c}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S6.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(\widetilde{G})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>G</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(\widetilde{G})</annotation></semantics></math> are matched; these weights can also be obtained directly from the Schur complement of the associated Laplacian matrix. The following is relevant for RN graphs:</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem10" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem10.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 6.10</span></span><span id="S6.Thmtheorem10.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, Prop. 3.25]</cite>)<span id="S6.Thmtheorem10.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem10.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmtheorem10.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem10.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S6.Thmtheorem10.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{G}=\operatorname{kron}_{x}(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>G</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>kron</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{G}=\operatorname{kron}_{x}(G)</annotation></semantics></math> with matched edge weights <math id="S6.Thmtheorem10.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>c</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{c}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then</span></p>
<table id="S6.Ex21" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{p}_{v}(\tilde{c})=p_{v}(c)+\frac{c_{xv}\cdot p_{x}(c)}{\sum_{xy\in E(G)}c_{xy}}\text{~if $v\in\partial x$,}\quad\quad\quad\tilde{p}_{v}(\tilde{c})=p_{v}(c)\text{~otherwise,}\quad\quad\text{~for $v\in V(\widetilde{G})$}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>v</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>c</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"> if </mtext><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">∈</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">,</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:3em;" width="3em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>v</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>c</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"> otherwise,</mtext></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"> for </mtext><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>G</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{p}_{v}(\tilde{c})=p_{v}(c)+\frac{c_{xv}\cdot p_{x}(c)}{\sum_{xy\in E(G)}c_{xy}}\text{~if $v\in\partial x$,}\quad\quad\quad\tilde{p}_{v}(\tilde{c})=p_{v}(c)\text{~otherwise,}\quad\quad\text{~for $v\in V(\widetilde{G})$}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In the above lemma, we note that if <math id="S6.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{v}(c),p_{x}(c)\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{v}(c),p_{x}(c)\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> then also <math id="S6.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{p}_{v}(\tilde{c})\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>v</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>c</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{p}_{v}(\tilde{c})\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, and similarly if all inequalities are strict. This leads to the following closure property:</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem11" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem11.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 6.11</span></span><span id="S6.Thmtheorem11.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem11.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmtheorem11.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem11.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If <math id="S6.Thmtheorem11.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is RN (resp. RP), then any Kron reduction of <math id="S6.Thmtheorem11.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is RN (resp. RP).</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof.</span>
Let <math id="S6.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> be an RN graph with edge weights <math id="S6.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S6.SS2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(c)\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(c)\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> and take any <math id="S6.SS2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\subset V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\subset V</annotation></semantics></math>. Then by taking Kron reductions of single vertices as in (<a href="#S6.E7" title="In Lemma 6.9 (quotient property, []). ‣ 6.2 Kron reduction ‣ 6 Graph operations ‣ Graphs with nonnegative resistance curvature" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>) with matched edge weights, and using Lemma <a href="#S6.Thmtheorem10" title="Lemma 6.10 ([, Prop. 3.25]). ‣ 6.2 Kron reduction ‣ 6 Graph operations ‣ Graphs with nonnegative resistance curvature" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.10</span></a>, we find that <math id="S6.SS2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{kron}_{U}(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>kron</mi><mi>U</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{kron}_{U}(G)</annotation></semantics></math> is again RN. The same derivation holds for RP graphs.
∎</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.3 </span>Circle inversion</h3>

<div id="S6.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Different from the other two operations, the next operation is defined with respect to a choice of edge weights; it originates from Fiedler’s matrix theory of simplices, where it corresponds to spherical inversion of a simplex around one of its vertices <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>, Rem. 3.4.5]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem12" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem12.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 6.12</span></span><span id="S6.Thmtheorem12.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(circle inversion, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>)<span id="S6.Thmtheorem12.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem12.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmtheorem12.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem12.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S6.Thmtheorem12.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> be an RN graph with vertex <math id="S6.Thmtheorem12.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in V</annotation></semantics></math> and weights <math id="S6.Thmtheorem12.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S6.Thmtheorem12.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(c)\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(c)\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>. The <em id="S6.Thmtheorem12.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">circle inversion over <math id="S6.Thmtheorem12.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math></em> is the graph obtained by removing <math id="S6.Thmtheorem12.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> and adding a new vertex <math id="S6.Thmtheorem12.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{x}</annotation></semantics></math> with edges <math id="S6.Thmtheorem12.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\hat{x}y\mid p_{y}(c)&gt;0\text{~in $G$}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"> in </mtext><mi>G</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\hat{x}y\mid p_{y}(c)&gt;0\text{~in $G$}\}</annotation></semantics></math>; we write <math id="S6.Thmtheorem12.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{cinv}_{x}(G,c)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>cinv</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{cinv}_{x}(G,c)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Similar to Kron reduction, circle inversion comes with a distinguished choice of edge weights. We make use of the effective resistance <math id="S6.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> defined as in (<a href="#S5.E5" title="In 5 Algebraic characterization ‣ Graphs with nonnegative resistance curvature" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>) between any pair of vertices.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem13" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem13.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 6.13</span></span><span id="S6.Thmtheorem13.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem13.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmtheorem13.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem13.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S6.Thmtheorem13.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>G</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{G}</annotation></semantics></math> be the circle inversion over <math id="S6.Thmtheorem13.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> with respect to weights <math id="S6.Thmtheorem13.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math>. The <em id="S6.Thmtheorem13.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">matched weights</em> <math id="S6.Thmtheorem13.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>c</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{c}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S6.Thmtheorem13.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>G</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{G}</annotation></semantics></math> are defined as</span></p>
<table id="S6.Ex22" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{c}_{\hat{x}y}=2\cdot p_{y}(c)\cdot\omega_{xy}\text{~if $y\in\partial\hat{x}$,}\quad\quad\tilde{c}_{uv}=c_{uv}\cdot\omega_{ux}\cdot\omega_{xv}\text{~if $u,v\not\in\partial\hat{x}$}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>c</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"> if </mtext><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">∈</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow></mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">,</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>c</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"> if </mtext><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">∉</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{c}_{\hat{x}y}=2\cdot p_{y}(c)\cdot\omega_{xy}\text{~if $y\in\partial\hat{x}$,}\quad\quad\tilde{c}_{uv}=c_{uv}\cdot\omega_{ux}\cdot\omega_{xv}\text{~if $u,v\not\in\partial\hat{x}$}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">These weights are only nonnegative if <math id="S6.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(c)\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(c)\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>. When fixing a choice of edge weights on a graph and keeping these weights matched under circle inversions, the map <math id="S6.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{cinv}_{\bullet}:(G,c)\mapsto(\widetilde{G},\tilde{c})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>cinv</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>G</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>c</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{cinv}_{\bullet}:(G,c)\mapsto(\widetilde{G},\tilde{c})</annotation></semantics></math> commutes and has an inverse, i.e., <math id="S6.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{cinv}_{x}\circ\operatorname{cinv}_{y}=\operatorname{cinv}_{y}\circ\operatorname{cinv}_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>cinv</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>cinv</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>cinv</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>cinv</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{cinv}_{x}\circ\operatorname{cinv}_{y}=\operatorname{cinv}_{y}\circ\operatorname{cinv}_{x}</annotation></semantics></math> up to relabeling vertices, and <math id="S6.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{cinv}_{\hat{x}}\circ\operatorname{cinv}_{x}=\operatorname{Id}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>cinv</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>cinv</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>Id</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{cinv}_{\hat{x}}\circ\operatorname{cinv}_{x}=\operatorname{Id}</annotation></semantics></math>. The relevance of circle inversion for RN graphs is due to the following result:</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem14" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem14.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 6.14</span></span><span id="S6.Thmtheorem14.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>, Thm. 3.4.4]</cite>)<span id="S6.Thmtheorem14.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem14.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmtheorem14.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem14.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S6.Thmtheorem14.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{G}=\operatorname{cinv}_{x}(G,c)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>G</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>cinv</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{G}=\operatorname{cinv}_{x}(G,c)</annotation></semantics></math> with matched edge weights <math id="S6.Thmtheorem14.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>c</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{c}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then</span></p>
<table id="S6.Ex23" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{p}_{v}(\tilde{c})=\tfrac{1}{2}\cdot c_{xv}\cdot\omega_{xv}\text{~if $v\in\partial x$,}\quad\quad\tilde{p}_{v}(\tilde{c})=p_{x}(c)\text{~if $v=\hat{x}$,}\quad\quad\tilde{p}_{v}(\tilde{c})=0\text{~otherwise,}\quad\text{~for $v\in V(\widetilde{G})$}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>v</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>c</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"> if </mtext><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">∈</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">,</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>v</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>c</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"> if </mtext><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>=</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">,</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>v</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>c</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"> otherwise,</mtext></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"> for </mtext><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>G</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{p}_{v}(\tilde{c})=\tfrac{1}{2}\cdot c_{xv}\cdot\omega_{xv}\text{~if $v\in\partial x$,}\quad\quad\tilde{p}_{v}(\tilde{c})=p_{x}(c)\text{~if $v=\hat{x}$,}\quad\quad\tilde{p}_{v}(\tilde{c})=0\text{~otherwise,}\quad\text{~for $v\in V(\widetilde{G})$}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">This directly leads to another closure property for RN and RP graphs.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem15" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem15.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 6.15</span></span><span id="S6.Thmtheorem15.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem15.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmtheorem15.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem15.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If <math id="S6.Thmtheorem15.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is RN, then any circle inversion of <math id="S6.Thmtheorem15.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is RN. Furthermore, if <math id="S6.Thmtheorem15.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{cinv}_{x}(G,c)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>cinv</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{cinv}_{x}(G,c)</annotation></semantics></math> is over a vertex <math id="S6.Thmtheorem15.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S6.Thmtheorem15.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{x}(c)&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{x}(c)&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> and which is connected to all other vertices, then it is RP.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S7" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">7 </span>Nonnegativity from submodularity</h2>

<div id="S7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We conclude the article with a speculative third approach to characterizing resistance nonnegativity. We again start from a space <math id="S7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}(G)</annotation></semantics></math> that parametrizes all possible effective resistances in a graph and conjecture that the condition <math id="S7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> translates to linear inequalities on <math id="S7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}(G)</annotation></semantics></math>; more precisely, we conjecture that these inequalities are those of the submodular cone.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For <math id="S7.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\subseteq V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\subseteq V</annotation></semantics></math> nonempty, we write <math id="S7.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega[U]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega[U]</annotation></semantics></math> for the submatrix of the resistance matrix <math id="S7.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega</annotation></semantics></math> with rows and columns in <math id="S7.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math>. A variant of the following set function was introduced in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, Ch. 3.4]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 7.1</span></span><span id="S7.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(resistance capacity)<span id="S7.Thmtheorem1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For a normalized graph <math id="S7.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, the <em id="S7.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">resistance capacity</em> is a set function <math id="S7.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{\bullet}(c):2^{V}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>V</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{\bullet}(c):2^{V}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> defined on the subsets of <math id="S7.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> as: <math id="S7.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{\emptyset}(c)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{\emptyset}(c)=0</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S7.Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{v}(c)=\tfrac{1}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{v}(c)=\tfrac{1}{2}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S7.Thmtheorem1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in V</annotation></semantics></math>, and</span></p>
<table id="S7.Ex24" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{U}(c)=\frac{1}{2}+\frac{1}{2}\Big(\sum_{u,v\in U}(\Omega[U]^{-1})_{uv}\Big)^{-1}\quad\quad\text{~for $U\subseteq V$ with $|U|\geq 2$.}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>U</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"> for </mtext><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"> with </mtext><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">.</mtext></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{U}(c)=\frac{1}{2}+\frac{1}{2}\Big(\sum_{u,v\in U}(\Omega[U]^{-1})_{uv}\Big)^{-1}\quad\quad\text{~for $U\subseteq V$ with $|U|\geq 2$.}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 7.2</span></span><span id="S7.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The resistance capacity <math id="S7.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>V</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{V}</annotation></semantics></math> is a discrete analogue of a well-studied invariant in potential theory on metric graphs, the so-called tau constant of a metric graph <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>, Thm. 1.27]</cite>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p3.1" class="ltx_p">A set function <math id="S7.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is called <em id="S7.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">monotone</em> if it satisfies <math id="S7.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(A)\leq f(B)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(A)\leq f(B)</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S7.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\subseteq B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\subseteq B</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 7.3</span></span><span id="S7.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For a normalized graph, the resistance capacity is monotone and lies in <math id="S7.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof.</span>
Monotonicity was proven in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, Prop 3.38]</cite>. For <math id="S7.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U=\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U=\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S7.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U=V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U=V</annotation></semantics></math> we directly compute <math id="S7.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{\emptyset}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{\emptyset}=0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S7.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{V}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>V</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{V}=1</annotation></semantics></math> by normalization; monotonicity then implies that <math id="S7.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{U}\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>U</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{U}\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S7.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\subseteq V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\subseteq V</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Next, we observe that the resistance capacity parametrizes all data of a weighted graph:</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 7.4</span></span><span id="S7.Thmtheorem4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The graph <math id="S7.Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> and edge weights <math id="S7.Thmtheorem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> can be recovered from <math id="S7.Thmtheorem4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\tau_{U}(c))_{U\subseteq V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>U</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\tau_{U}(c))_{U\subseteq V}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof.</span>
For <math id="S7.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U=uv" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U=uv</annotation></semantics></math>, the value of <math id="S7.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> was computed in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, p53]</cite> to be <math id="S7.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{uv}(c)=\tfrac{1}{2}+\tfrac{1}{4}\omega_{uv}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{uv}(c)=\tfrac{1}{2}+\tfrac{1}{4}\omega_{uv}</annotation></semantics></math>. We can thus recover all effective resistances in the graph as <math id="S7.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{uv}=4\tau_{uv}(c)-2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{uv}=4\tau_{uv}(c)-2</annotation></semantics></math>. From this data, the graph and edge weights can be found by constructing the Laplacian matrix via <math id="S7.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=\Omega^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=\Omega^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math> as in (<a href="#S5.E6" title="In 5 Algebraic characterization ‣ Graphs with nonnegative resistance curvature" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>).
∎</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p7.1" class="ltx_p">We thus have a third parametrization in addition to <math id="S7.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(G)^{\circ}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∘</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(G)^{\circ}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S7.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{K}(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{K}(G)</annotation></semantics></math>: the set of resistance capacities <math id="S7.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\tau_{U}(c))_{U\subseteq V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>U</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\tau_{U}(c))_{U\subseteq V}</annotation></semantics></math> for all normalized edge weights <math id="S7.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> on a given graph <math id="S7.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>. We denote this set by <math id="S7.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}(G)\subseteq[0,1]^{2^{V}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>V</mi></msup></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}(G)\subseteq[0,1]^{2^{V}}</annotation></semantics></math>. We now relate certain inequalities on this space to resistance nonnegativity. A set function <math id="S7.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:2^{V}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>V</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:2^{V}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> is called <em id="S7.p7.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">submodular</em> if it satisfies</p>
<table id="S7.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(A)+f(B)\geq f(A\cup B)+f(A\cap B)\quad\quad\quad\text{~for all $A,B\subseteq V$.}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∪</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∩</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:3em;" width="3em"></mspace><mrow><mtext> for all </mtext><mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(A)+f(B)\geq f(A\cup B)+f(A\cap B)\quad\quad\quad\text{~for all $A,B\subseteq V$.}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.p7.2" class="ltx_p">The set of all submodular set functions on a given ground set <math id="S7.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> is called the submodular cone; it is a polyhedral cone in <math id="S7.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{2^{V}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>V</mi></msup></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{2^{V}}</annotation></semantics></math> cut out by the submodularity inequalities (<a href="#S7.E8" title="In 7 Nonnegativity from submodularity ‣ Graphs with nonnegative resistance curvature" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>). We rephrase <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, Thm. 6.33]</cite>, which implies submodularity for the resistance curvature when <math id="S7.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(c)\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(c)\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 7.5</span></span><span id="S7.Thmtheorem5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S7.Thmtheorem5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S7.Thmtheorem5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> be normalized with <math id="S7.Thmtheorem5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(c)\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(c)\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S7.Thmtheorem5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{\bullet}(c)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{\bullet}(c)</annotation></semantics></math> is submodular.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof.</span>
Let <math id="S7.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{2}_{U}=\tau_{U}-\tfrac{1}{2}\delta_{U\neq\emptyset}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>U</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>U</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{2}_{U}=\tau_{U}-\tfrac{1}{2}\delta_{U\neq\emptyset}</annotation></semantics></math> be the resistance capacity shifted by <math id="S7.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-\tfrac{1}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-\tfrac{1}{2}</annotation></semantics></math> for all sets except for the empty set <math id="S7.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U=\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U=\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math>. It was shown in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, Thm. 6.33]</cite> that <math id="S7.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies the submodularity inequality (<a href="#S7.E8" title="In 7 Nonnegativity from submodularity ‣ Graphs with nonnegative resistance curvature" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>) whenever the two sets <math id="S7.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A,B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A,B</annotation></semantics></math> intersect. The main obstruction in proving full submodularity of <math id="S7.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is that inequality (<a href="#S7.E8" title="In 7 Nonnegativity from submodularity ‣ Graphs with nonnegative resistance curvature" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>) fails for distinct singletons <math id="S7.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A=\{a\},B=\{b\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A=\{a\},B=\{b\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Indeed, we have</p>
<table id="S7.Ex25" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{2}_{a}(c)+\sigma^{2}_{b}(c)=0&lt;\sigma^{2}_{ab}(c)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>b</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{2}_{a}(c)+\sigma^{2}_{b}(c)=0&lt;\sigma^{2}_{ab}(c).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.p8.2" class="ltx_p">This obstruction is no longer present for <math id="S7.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> due to the <math id="S7.p8.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-\tfrac{1}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-\tfrac{1}{2}</annotation></semantics></math> shift and normalization; we find</p>
<table id="S7.Ex26" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{a}(c)+\tau_{b}(c)=1=\tau_{V}(c)\geq\tau_{ab}(c)\quad\quad\text{~for distinct $a,b\in V$}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>V</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mrow><mtext> for distinct </mtext><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{a}(c)+\tau_{b}(c)=1=\tau_{V}(c)\geq\tau_{ab}(c)\quad\quad\text{~for distinct $a,b\in V$}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.p8.3" class="ltx_p">All other cases are then dealt with inductively in the proof of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, Thm. 6.33]</cite>.
∎</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_conjecture">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Conjecture 7.6</span></span><span id="S7.Thmtheorem6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S7.Thmtheorem6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S7.Thmtheorem6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> be normalized with <math id="S7.Thmtheorem6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{\bullet}(c)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{\bullet}(c)</annotation></semantics></math> submodular. Then <math id="S7.Thmtheorem6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(c)\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(c)\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p9.1" class="ltx_p">The author proved the partial result “submodular <math id="S7.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> implies <math id="S7.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(c)\geq-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(c)\geq-1</annotation></semantics></math>” in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, Prop. 6.34]</cite>. Conjecture <a href="#S7.Thmtheorem6" title="Conjecture 7.6. ‣ 7 Nonnegativity from submodularity ‣ Graphs with nonnegative resistance curvature" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.6</span></a> is equivalent to the following conjecture on resistance nonnegative graphs:</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_conjecture">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Conjecture 7.7</span></span><span id="S7.Thmtheorem7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">A graph <math id="S7.Thmtheorem7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is RN if and only if <math id="S7.Thmtheorem7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}(G)</annotation></semantics></math> intersects the submodular cone in <math id="S7.Thmtheorem7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,1]^{2^{V}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>V</mi></msup></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,1]^{2^{V}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Acknowledgements:</span> We thank Harry Richman for pointing out mistakes in an earlier version of the manuscript.</p>
</div>
</section>
